OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026", "title": "The Unique Games Theorem", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "102", "family_title": "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds", "abstract_html": "We prove the Unique Games Conjecture. For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\), we give a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from 3SAT to Unique Games over a fixed finite alphabet, with completeness at least \\(1-\\varepsilon\\) and soundness at most <i>δ</i>.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Unique Games Theorem)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">A Unique Games instance has a finite vertex set, a finite alphabet \\(K\\), and a nonempty list of oriented constraints \\(e=(u_e,v_e,\\pi_e)\\), where \\(\\pi_e\\) is a permutation of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A labeling \\(a\\) satisfies \\(e\\) when \\(a(v_e)=\\pi_e(a(u_e))\\). Its value is the fraction of satisfied constraints; \\(\\operatorname{val}(G)\\) is the maximum labeling value.</p><p class=\"li\">“Explicit unweighted” means that the entire vertex and constraint lists, including every permutation table, are written out.</p><p class=\"li\">A simple bipartite instance has two disjoint vertex sets and at most one constraint on each pair of opposite vertices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\), there are an integer \\(s\\ge1\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to explicit unweighted Unique Games instances \\(G_\\varphi\\) over \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^s\\) such that\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ satisfiable}\\implies \\operatorname{val}(G_\\varphi)\\ge1-\\varepsilon,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ unsatisfiable}\\implies \\operatorname{val}(G_\\varphi)\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nThe graph is simple and bipartite, and every constraint is a translation\n\\[\na(v_e)=a(u_e)+c_e.\n\\]\nThe alphabet, the degree of the running-time polynomial, and its constants depend only on \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\), and choose positive rationals \\(\\varepsilon_0\\le\\varepsilon\\), \\(\\delta_0\\le\\delta\\).</p><p>If a matrix game \\(H\\) has NO value at most \\(99/100\\), Proposition 10 with \\(\\tau\\le1/200\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(B)\n\\le1-\\frac{1/100-\\tau}{4}\n\\le1-\\frac1{800}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nThis alphabet-independent gap permits choosing repetition before the alphabet. Choose \\(t\\ge1\\) with\n\\[\n\\left(1-\\frac1{10240000}\\right)^t\\le\\delta_0. \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nFor example, \\(t\\ge\\lceil10240000\\log(1/\\delta_0)\\rceil\\) suffices. Proposition 7 gives the required soundness amplification.</p><p>Choose rational\n\\[\np_*\\le\\varepsilon_0/t,\\qquad\n\\tau\\le\\min\\{1/200,\\varepsilon_0/t\\}. \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nUse Proposition 1 to obtain \\(r_*\\). Set \\(\\eta=2^{-r_*}/16\\); choose \\(\\alpha,r_s\\) from Proposition 4, using a positive rational lower bound for \\(\\alpha\\). Choose sufficiently large \\(\\ell\\) satisfying (5.4), and construct the corresponding latent data \\(V=K\\oplus W,C,\\mu\\).</p><p>All choices are effective by Propositions 1 and 4. Next choose a cube \\(k\\ge8\\) sufficiently large for Proposition 9. This can be done by enumerating cubes and checking the proof’s estimates. With \\(m=1+2k\\), \\(\\beta=k^{-2/3}\\), require \\(m\\ge m_0(\\eta,\\ell)\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha^2}{32}2^{(\\ell-r_s)(m-r_s)}\n&gt;(2r_s+1)(\\ell+m).\n\\]\nFor witness transfer, require\n\\[\n\\frac14\\left[\n\\left(1+\\frac{|V|-1}{3}\\beta^2\\right)^k-1\n\\right]\n&lt;\n\\frac{\\gamma_1^2}{(1+\\alpha/4)^2}.\n\\]\nFor the final contradiction require\n\\[\n\\left(1-\\frac{\\beta\\,2^{-\\dim W-r_s}}{3600}\\right)^k&lt;\\gamma.\n\\]\nThese rational conditions hold for sufficiently large cubes and depend only on the prescribed errors.</p><p>Finally choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\xi&lt;\\min\\{1/100,\\varepsilon_0/(2kt)\\}. \\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nThus \\(r_*\\) precedes \\(\\ell\\), advice dimensions precede \\(k\\), and \\(k\\) precedes \\(\\xi\\).</p><p>Given a \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) formula, use Proposition 2 and construct the matrix game \\(H\\) of Proposition 3. Proposition 9 gives\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{val}(H)\\ge1-d,\n\\quad d=k\\xi+p_*/2\\le\\varepsilon_0/t,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{val}(H)\\le99/100.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 10 to get simple unweighted bipartite \\(B\\), with\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{val}(B)\\ge1-(d+\\tau)/4,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{val}(B)\\le1-1/800.\n\\]\nOutput \\(G=B^{\\otimes t}\\). Proposition 7 and (7.4) give NO value at most \\(\\delta_0\\le\\delta\\). A coordinatewise YES labeling and a union bound give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\n\\ge1-\\frac{t(d+\\tau)}4\n\\ge1-\\varepsilon_0/2\n\\ge1-\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>The two sides of \\(G\\) are the \\(t\\)-fold products of the sides of \\(B\\). A pair of product vertices determines at most one edge in each coordinate, hence at most one product edge. Uniform independent edge sampling is uniform on product edges. The product of translations on \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) is translation on \\(K^t=\\mathbb F_2^{\\ell t}\\); take \\(s=\\ell t\\).</p><p>For encoding, if the preprocessed parity instance has \\(N_E\\) occurrences, there are \\(N_E^k\\) equation tuples. The numbers of tables, noise outcomes, and selectors per tuple are fixed constants. Thus the number \\(h\\) of listed matrix occurrences is a fixed constant times \\(N_E^k\\). Weights are products of \\(k\\) parity weights and fixed rational factors, hence have polynomial bit length.</p><p>Exact keys and representatives are computable in fixed dimensions; even pairwise key comparison is polynomial. There are at most \\(2h\\) appearing endpoints. Rounding uses \\(Q=\\lceil h/\\tau\\rceil=O(h)\\), subdivision gives \\(4Q\\) edges, and the final game has \\((4Q)^t\\) edges. Since all dimensions, \\(k,t\\), and the final alphabet are fixed, full permutation tables and endpoint indices have polynomial total encoding length. The reduction is deterministic polynomial-time.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Section 8.1, unnumbered Max-Cut and Vertex Cover consequences", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Max-Cut maximizes the number of edges crossing a bipartition.</p><p class=\"li\">Vertex Cover minimizes the number of vertices meeting every edge.</p><p class=\"li\">For a maximization problem, approximation ratio \\(r\\) means returning value at least \\(r\\) times the optimum.</p><p class=\"li\">For a minimization problem, approximation factor \\(c\\) means returning value at most \\(c\\) times the optimum.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}\\simeq0.87856\\) be the Goemans–Williamson constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Max-Cut approximation at every fixed ratio \\(r\\in(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}},1)\\) is NP-hard. Vertex Cover approximation at every fixed factor \\(c\\in(1,2)\\) is NP-hard.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 supplies the ordinary edge-value Unique Games hypothesis, with the two errors fixed before the alphabet.</p><p>For Max-Cut, apply Khot–Kindler–Mossel–O’Donnell [24, Theorem 1], using the Majority Is Stablest theorem [32]. Their reduction gives NP-hardness at every fixed ratio strictly greater than \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}\\) and less than one.</p><p>For Vertex Cover, apply Khot–Regev [27]. Where the strengthened bipartite formulation is required, Proposition 11 converts Theorem 1’s instances to simple common-left-degree games whose YES labeling satisfies every edge at most left vertices, with arbitrarily prescribed fixed errors. Their reduction gives NP-hardness at every fixed factor strictly between one and two.</p><p>The approximation thresholds and conditional reductions are those of the cited authors.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 8.1 (Raghavendra’s consequence)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\) is a finite nonempty domain, and \\(\\Lambda\\) is a finite family of Boolean predicates on \\(D\\), of bounded arity.</p><p class=\"li\">A weighted \\(\\Lambda\\)-instance has an explicitly listed finite variable set and predicate occurrences with nonnegative binary-encoded rational weights summing to one.</p><p class=\"li\">An assignment maps the variables to \\(D\\); \\(\\operatorname{OPT}\\) is its maximum satisfied weight.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\operatorname{SDP}_R\\) denotes Raghavendra’s relaxation as defined in [37, Section 4.5].</p><p class=\"li\">\\(J\\) is a fixed \\(\\Lambda\\)-instance with\n\\[\nA=\\operatorname{OPT}(J)&lt;B=\\operatorname{SDP}_R(J).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;(B-A)/2\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish \\(\\Lambda\\)-instances \\(I\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(I)\\ge B-\\eta\n\\]\nfrom those satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(I)\\le A+\\eta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the common-arity convention of [37, Definitions 7.2.1–7.2.2], identify \\(D\\) with a fixed \\([q]\\), pad predicates by ignored trailing arguments to a common positive arity, and fill those positions with one fixed variable of \\(J\\). Such a variable exists because \\(A&lt;B\\). Applied payoff dependence sets are unchanged, preserving both \\(\\operatorname{OPT}\\) and the relaxation. When outputting occurrences, discard only the added positions.</p><p>Apply Raghavendra’s fixed-instance theorem [37, Theorem 7.9], with tolerance \\(\\eta/2\\); see also [36, Theorem 1.1 and Lemma 2.3]. The witness \\(J\\) and tolerance fix strengthened Unique Games errors. Theorem 1 and Proposition 11 supply this premise with a fixed alphabet \\([M]\\). The imported verifier gap is\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{OPT}&gt;B-\\eta/2,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{OPT}\\le A+\\eta/2.\n\\]</p><p>For this fixed invocation, [37, Sections 7.3.1 and 7.5] specifies a local law \\(\\theta\\) on a finite set \\(S\\). A record specifies an applied predicate, its \\(a(s)\\) ordered oracle-query strings in \\(D^M\\), and positions filled by their replies. This law depends only on fixed \\(J,\\eta,M\\). Choose a fixed rational law \\(\\widehat\\theta\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac12\\sum_{s\\in S}|\\widehat\\theta(s)-\\theta(s)|&lt;\\eta/2.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(L_\\Phi,V_\\Phi\\) be the sides of the nonempty simple game from Proposition 11, with common left degree \\(h\\). List output variables \\(V_\\Phi\\times D^M\\). For every record \\(s\\), left vertex \\(u\\), and ordered neighbor tuple in \\(N(u)^{a(s)}\\), emit the prescribed \\(\\Lambda\\)-predicate on the edge-permuted query variables, with weight\n\\[\n\\frac{\\widehat\\theta(s)}{|L_\\Phi|h^{a(s)}}.\n\\]\nFill any syntactic positions independent of the payoff with the first output variable. The weights are rational and sum to one.</p><p>The number of variables is \\(|V_\\Phi||D|^M\\), and the number of occurrences is\n\\[\n|L_\\Phi|\\sum_{s\\in S}h^{a(s)}.\n\\]\nAll local parameters are fixed, so explicit neighbor lists and permutations give polynomial enumeration and polynomial-bit weights.</p><p>For any assignment \\(\\sigma\\), let \\(g_\\sigma(s)\\in[0,1]\\) be conditional acceptance over outer choices. Then\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{val}_{\\widehat\\theta}(\\sigma)\n-\\operatorname{val}_{\\theta}(\\sigma)|\n\\le\\frac12\\sum_s|\\widehat\\theta(s)-\\theta(s)|\n&lt;\\eta/2.\n\\]\nTaking maxima preserves the bound. The rationalized YES optimum is at least \\(B-\\eta\\), and the NO optimum at most \\(A+\\eta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 8.2 (Ordering-CSP consequence)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(\\varnothing\\ne\\Pi\\subsetneq S_k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite weighted \\(\\Pi\\)-ordering-CSP instance has an explicitly listed finite variable set and a finite list of ordered \\(k\\)-tuples of distinct variables. Its nonnegative binary-encoded rational occurrence weights sum to one.</p><p class=\"li\">A linear order satisfies \\((v_1,\\ldots,v_k)\\) when\n\\[\nv_{\\pi(1)}\\prec\\cdots\\prec v_{\\pi(k)}\n\\quad\\text{for some }\\pi\\in\\Pi.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\operatorname{OPT}_\\Pi(I)\\) for the maximum satisfied weight and \\(\\rho_\\Pi=|\\Pi|/k!\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;(1-\\rho_\\Pi)/2\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish instances satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_\\Pi(I)\\ge1-\\varepsilon\n\\]\nfrom those satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_\\Pi(I)\\le\\rho_\\Pi+\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nConsequently, approximation at every fixed ratio \\(r\\in(\\rho_\\Pi,1]\\) is NP-hard. A random order, derandomizable by conditional expectation, achieves ratio \\(\\rho_\\Pi\\), giving the optimal threshold \\(\\rho_\\Pi\\).</p><p>In particular, the thresholds are \\(1/2\\) for finite weighted Maximum Acyclic Subgraph and \\(1/3\\) for finite weighted Betweenness.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 12 with \\(\\delta=\\varepsilon\\). Its required errors \\(\\zeta,\\nu\\), divisibility \\(q\\mid h\\), and degree lower bound \\(H\\) are fixed before the Unique Games alphabet. Theorem 1 followed by Proposition 11, with \\(d=q\\), supplies the required simple strong-left game. Composition gives the displayed ordering gap.</p><p>For \\(r&gt;\\rho_\\Pi\\), choose the positive error smaller if necessary so that\n\\[\n\\varepsilon&lt;\\frac{r-\\rho_\\Pi}{1+r}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nr(1-\\varepsilon)&gt;\\rho_\\Pi+\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nand an \\(r\\)-approximation distinguishes the gap.</p><p>A uniform random order satisfies each distinct-variable occurrence with probability \\(\\rho_\\Pi\\), so it achieves expected value at least \\(\\rho_\\Pi\\operatorname{OPT}\\). Derandomize by exposing an order prefix. If \\(s\\) variables of an occurrence have appeared, its conditional success probability is the number of permutations in \\(\\Pi\\) whose first \\(s\\) positions are their exposed roles, divided by \\((k-s)!\\). Inspect the fixed \\(S_k\\), sum against rational weights, and choose a next variable preserving conditional expectation. The explicit variable list makes the number of choices polynomial; denominator bit lengths and comparisons remain polynomial.</p><p>For Maximum Acyclic Subgraph, take \\(k=2,\\Pi=\\{12\\}\\). Forward edges form an acyclic subgraph, and an acyclic subgraph has a topological order, giving threshold \\(1/2\\).</p><p>For Betweenness, take \\(k=3,\\Pi=\\{123,321\\}\\). Its random-order threshold is \\(2/3!=1/3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Corollary 8.3 (Cut, deletion, and clustering hardness)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">For Weighted Multicut, \\(G=(V,E)\\) is a finite undirected graph with positive rational edge costs \\(c_e\\), and \\(\\mathcal D\\) consists of \\(k\\ge1\\) distinct demand pairs with unequal endpoints. For \\(M\\subseteq E\\), let \\(c(M)=\\sum_{e\\in M}c_e\\), and let \\(q_{\\mathcal D}(M)\\) count demand pairs disconnected in \\(G-M\\). The objective is to minimize \\(c(M)\\) subject to \\(q_{\\mathcal D}(M)=k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The nonuniform Sparsest Cut search problem minimizes \\(c(M)/q_{\\mathcal D}(M)\\) over cutsets with \\(q_{\\mathcal D}(M)&gt;0\\). Only the listed demand pairs have unit demand.</p><p class=\"li\">Weighted Min-\\(2\\mathrm{CNF}^{\\equiv}\\) Deletion has positive rationally weighted clauses \\((\\ell\\equiv\\ell')\\), where the literals express equality or disequality of variables; an assignment pays the total weight of false clauses.</p><p class=\"li\">Arbitrary weighted-graph Correlation Clustering has signed present edges with nonnegative rational weights. A partition pays for positive edges between clusters and negative edges within clusters. Missing edges carry no penalty.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed real \\(C&gt;1\\), approximating each of these four minimization problems within factor \\(C\\) is NP-hard.</p><p>For Sparsest Cut this is hardness of the search task under polynomial-time Cook reductions: an algorithm must return a cutset, not merely estimate the optimum. Consequently, unless \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\), none of the four problems admits a deterministic polynomial-time approximation with any fixed constant factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Chawla–Krauthgamer–Kumar–Rabani–Sivakumar [9, Corollary 1.3] give the first three hardness conclusions under the ordinary edge-value Unique Games hypothesis. For requested \\(C&gt;1\\), invoke their statement at any fixed \\(L&gt;C\\): a \\(C\\)-approximation would also be an \\(L\\)-approximation.</p><p>Theorem 1 supplies the hypothesis. Orient its bipartite constraints left-to-right, renumber its alphabet as \\([d]\\), \\(d=2^s\\ge2\\), and give edges equal weights summing to one. Pad the smaller question side by isolated questions if required; this preserves value and polynomial size. The Sparsest Cut search qualification is [9, Section 1.3].</p><p>For Correlation Clustering, the hard Multicut instances of [9, Section 2.1] have distinct antipodal demand pairs within \\(d\\)-cubes; graph edges are cube edges or join different cubes. Since \\(d\\ge2\\), these demand pairs are nonedges. The transformation of Demaine–Emanuel–Fiat–Immorlica [10, Theorem 4.7 and Corollary 4.8] therefore introduces no opposite-signed parallel edges. It preserves optimum and converts every clustering into a multicut of no greater cost. The fixed-factor hardness transfers.</p><p>The separate uniform-demand Sparsest Cut result [33] and the kernel-clustering application [35] are companion-manuscript results, not inputs to these proofs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 (Latent alphabet gadget)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conventions.", "statement": "<p>Conventions.\nAll spaces and linear maps are over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), except where a larger field is specified. For \\(G\\le V^*\\) and \\(K\\le V\\), \\(G|_K\\) is the image under restriction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(p_*&gt;0\\), there is an integer \\(r_*\\) such that, for every sufficiently large integer \\(\\ell\\), there exist a finite-dimensional binary space \\(V\\) containing an identified subspace \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\), a map \\(C:V\\to K\\), and a rational probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on \\(V\\), satisfying\n\\[\nC(x+h)=C(x)+h\\qquad(x\\in V,\\ h\\in K), \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Pr_{x\\text{ uniform},\\,a\\sim\\mu}[C(x+a)\\ne C(x)]\\le p_*, \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nand, for every \\(G\\le V^*\\) with \\(\\dim(G|_K)\\ge r_*\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_{a\\sim\\mu}[\\exists g\\in G:\\ g(a)\\ne0]\\ge\\frac18. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nThe samples \\(x,a\\) are independent. The threshold \\(r_*\\) is fixed before \\(\\ell\\). For rational \\(p_*\\), the threshold and all finite data can be found by a terminating deterministic algorithm.</p>", "proof": "<p>First realize the fixed-\\(B\\) shift action by an actual subspace. Put\n\\[\nV_0=P_s/\\ker\\lambda_s,\n\\]\nwhere the kernel is taken inside \\(R_s\\le P_s\\). Let \\(q_0:P_s\\to V_0\\) be the quotient. The shift identity makes \\(C_s\\) invariant under this kernel, so it descends to \\(\\overline C:V_0\\to B\\). Identify \\(R_s/\\ker\\lambda_s\\) with \\(B\\). Then\n\\[\n\\overline C(x+b')=\\overline C(x)+b'.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\nu\\) be the pushforward of \\(\\xi_s\\). Uniform input pushes to uniform input, preserving change probability \\(p_s\\).</p><p>If \\(G_0\\le V_0^*\\) restricts onto \\(B^*\\), choose a linear section \\(\\sigma:B^*\\to G_0\\). Then \\(\\psi_z=\\sigma(z)\\circ q_0\\) is a full lift, since\n\\[\n\\psi_z(h)=z(\\lambda_s h)\\qquad(h\\in R_s).\n\\]\nLemma 1.7 yields\n\\[\n\\Pr_{a\\sim\\nu}[\\exists g\\in G_0:g(a)\\ne0]\\ge\\frac14. \\tag{3.22}\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(r_0\\) sufficiently large that\n\\[\n\\exp(1-H_{r_0}/8)\\le p_*,\n\\]\nthen choose \\(\\epsilon_0,d,b,\\theta,s\\) as above. Lemma 1.5 gives\n\\[\np_s\\le(1-\\theta)^s\n\\le e^{-\\theta s}\n\\le e^{\\theta-H_{r_0}/8}\n\\le p_*.\n\\]\nSet \\(r_*=b+2\\).</p><p>For any \\(\\ell\\ge b+2\\), let \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\), and let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be all binary injections \\(L:B\\to K\\). On\n\\[\nP=\\prod_{L\\in\\mathcal L}V_0\n\\]\ndefine\n\\[\n\\widetilde C((x_L)_L)=\\sum_L L\\overline C(x_L).\n\\]\nThe product shift space \\(\\mathcal T=\\prod_L B\\) acts through\n\\[\n\\Lambda:\\mathcal T\\to K,\\qquad \\Lambda((b_L)_L)=\\sum_L Lb_L.\n\\]\nThis map is onto, because every nonzero vector of \\(K\\) lies in the image of some injection. Quotient \\(P\\) by \\(\\ker\\Lambda\\) inside \\(\\mathcal T\\). The resulting \\(V\\) contains \\(\\mathcal T/\\ker\\Lambda=K\\), and \\(\\widetilde C\\) descends to the equivariant \\(C\\).</p><p>Choose a uniform copy \\(L\\) and update only that copy by independent \\(\\nu\\)-noise; push this law to \\(V\\), obtaining \\(\\mu\\). Injectivity of \\(L\\) shows that the nonlinear output changes exactly when the selected copy changes. Thus its change probability remains \\(p_s\\le p_*\\).</p><p>For \\(G\\le V^*\\), let \\(S=G|_K\\), with \\(r=\\dim S\\ge b+2\\). Pull \\(G\\) back and restrict it to copy \\(L\\), obtaining \\(G_L\\le V_0^*\\). Its restriction to \\(B\\) is \\(L^*S\\). Failure of \\(L^*:S\\to B^*\\) to be onto is equivalent to some nonzero \\(b'\\in B\\) satisfying \\(Lb'\\in S^\\perp\\). For fixed \\(b'\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_L[Lb'\\in S^\\perp]\n=\\frac{2^{\\ell-r}-1}{2^\\ell-1}\\le2^{-r}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\Pr_L[L^*S\\ne B^*]\\le(2^b-1)2^{-r}\\le\\frac14.\n\\]\nOn every other copy, (3.22) gives detection at least \\(1/4\\). Averaging gives at least \\(3/16\\ge1/8\\).</p><p>All noise laws are pushforwards of uniform finite choices and therefore rational. For effectiveness, choose \\(m\\ge0\\) with \\(2^{-m}\\le p_*\\), and then \\(r_0\\) with \\(H_{r_0}\\ge8(m+1)\\). Choose rational \\(\\epsilon_0\\) satisfying (3.11). Enumerate fields with \\(3d\\ge r_0\\), their binary subspaces, and the maps in genericity’s definition until the nongeneric fractions at ranks \\(1,\\ldots,r_0\\) are all at most \\(\\epsilon_0\\). Lemma 1.4 ensures termination. Products, isomorphisms, injections, quotients, function tables, and rational laws are then obtained by finite enumeration and binary linear algebra.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions for the quadratic block.", "statement": "<p>Definitions for the quadratic block.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(r_0\\ge1\\), and write \\(H_0=0\\), \\(H_r=\\sum_{j=1}^r1/j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F=\\mathbb F_{2^d}\\), \\(q=2^d\\), and \\(X=B=F^3\\), regarded as binary spaces of dimension \\(b=3d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(X^*\\) and \\(B^*\\) with \\(F^3\\) using the trace pairing \\(z(y)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{F/\\mathbb F_2}(z\\cdot y)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nQ(x_1,x_2,x_3)=(x_2x_3,x_1x_3,x_1x_2),\n\\]\n\\[\nD(x,a)=Q(x+a)+Q(x)+Q(a),\n\\]\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{blk}}(x,y)=y+Q(x).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a one-dimensional \\(F\\)-subspace \\(A=Fv\\le F^3\\), define\n\\[\nD_A=\\{y\\in B:v\\cdot y=0\\},\n\\qquad\nU_A=\\{(a,Q(a)+w):a\\in A,\\ w\\in D_A\\}.\n\\]\nThese definitions are independent of the nonzero generator \\(v\\). Put\n\\[\n\\theta=\\frac1{q^2+q+1}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every line \\(A\\), \\(U_A\\) is binary linear of dimension \\(b\\). For \\(a\\in A,x\\in X\\), \\(D(x,a)\\in D_A\\); for \\(a,a'\\in A\\), \\(D(a,a')=0\\). The trace annihilator of \\(D_A\\) is \\(A\\), and the spaces \\(U_A\\) together span \\(X\\oplus B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The coordinates of \\(D(x,a)\\) are\n\\[\nx_2a_3+a_2x_3,\\qquad x_1a_3+a_1x_3,\\qquad x_1a_2+a_1x_2.\n\\]\nWhen \\(a=tv\\), every term in \\(v\\cdot D(x,a)\\) occurs twice. When both arguments are multiples of \\(v\\), each polar coordinate is zero. Thus \\(Q|_A\\) is binary additive, and the parametrization of \\(U_A\\) by \\(A\\oplus D_A\\) is a binary linear bijection, of dimension \\(d+2d=b\\).</p><p>Every \\(z\\in A\\) annihilates \\(D_A\\). The annihilator has binary dimension \\(3d-2d=d\\), so it is exactly \\(A\\).</p><p>For each coordinate line \\(A=Fe_i\\), \\(U_A\\) contains \\((Fe_i,0)\\), since \\(Q(te_i)=0\\), and also \\(\\{0\\}\\oplus D_A\\). The three coordinate lines and their vertical hyperplanes span \\(X\\oplus B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Response to one block update)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the quadratic-block setting of Lemma 1.1.", "statement": "<p>Assume the quadratic-block setting of Lemma 1.1. Fix a line \\(A\\). Let \\((x,y)\\) be uniform on \\(X\\oplus B\\), and independently let \\(\\zeta\\) be uniform on \\(U_A\\setminus\\{0\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr[C_{\\mathrm{blk}}((x,y)+\\zeta)\\ne C_{\\mathrm{blk}}(x,y)]=1-\\theta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For an update \\((a,Q(a)+w)\\in U_A\\),\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{blk}}((x,y)+(a,Q(a)+w))+C_{\\mathrm{blk}}(x,y)\n=w+D(x,a).\n\\]\nFirst sample the update uniformly from all of \\(U_A\\). Its parameters \\(a\\in A,w\\in D_A\\) are independent and uniform. For fixed \\(x,a\\), the output difference is uniform on \\(D_A\\), and hence is nonzero with probability \\(1-q^{-2}\\).</p><p>The zero update has mass \\(q^{-3}\\) and never changes the output. Conditioning on a nonzero update gives\n\\[\n\\frac{1-q^{-2}}{1-q^{-3}}\n=\\frac{q(q+1)}{q^2+q+1}\n=1-\\theta.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Kernel of a block restriction)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the quadratic-block setting of Lemma 1.1.", "statement": "<p>Assume the quadratic-block setting of Lemma 1.1. Let \\(S\\le B^*\\), and let \\(\\gamma:S\\to X^*\\) be binary linear. Write\n\\[\n\\gamma_z(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(g(z)\\cdot x)\n\\]\nfor the associated binary linear map \\(g:S\\to F^3\\). Define\n\\[\n\\kappa_{A,\\gamma}:S\\to U_A^*,\n\\qquad\n\\kappa_{A,\\gamma}(z)=(\\gamma_z,z)|_{U_A}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every nonzero \\(z\\in S\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa_{A,\\gamma}(z)=0\n\\iff\nA=Fz\\ \\text{ and }\\ z\\cdot g(z)=\\sqrt{z_1z_2z_3}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(z\\) lies in the kernel, it annihilates every vertical vector \\((0,w)\\) with \\(w\\in D_A\\). Lemma 1.1 gives \\(z\\in A\\), so \\(A=Fz\\).</p><p>On the remaining vectors \\((tz,Q(tz))\\), the functional has value\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(tg(z)\\cdot z+t^2z_1z_2z_3\\bigr). \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nThe three terms in \\(z\\cdot Q(tz)\\) are equal, and three equals one in characteristic two. Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(t^2c)=\\operatorname{Tr}(t\\sqrt c),\n\\]\nnondegeneracy of the trace pairing shows that (3.7) vanishes for every \\(t\\) precisely when\n\\[\nz\\cdot g(z)=\\sqrt{z_1z_2z_3}.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(z\\in A\\), this condition is also sufficient.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Definition 3.5 and Lemma 3.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume \\(F=\\mathbb F_{2^d}\\) and \\(B=F^3\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume \\(F=\\mathbb F_{2^d}\\) and \\(B=F^3\\). A binary \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace \\(S\\le F^3\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), is generic if:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. no two distinct nonzero elements of \\(S\\) are proportional over \\(F\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. for every binary linear \\(g:S\\to F^3\\), at most \\(3r\\) nonzero \\(z\\in S\\) satisfy\n\\[\nz\\cdot g(z)=\\sqrt{z_1z_2z_3}. \\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed \\(r\\ge1\\), a uniformly random binary \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace of \\(F^3\\) is generic with probability tending to one as \\(d\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Moreover, for a fixed extension map \\(\\gamma\\), a uniform line \\(A\\), and \\(r_A=\\dim\\operatorname{im}\\kappa_{A,\\gamma}\\), a generic rank-\\(r\\) space satisfies\n\\[\nr-r_A\\le1,\\qquad\n\\Pr[r_A&lt;r]\\le3r\\theta,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_A[H_r-H_{r_A}]\\le3\\theta. \\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nFor an arbitrary rank-\\(r\\) space,\n\\[\n\\Pr[r_A&lt;r]\\le(2^r-1)\\theta\\le2^r\\theta,\n\\qquad H_r-H_{r_A}\\le H_r. \\tag{3.10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose independent uniform \\(x_{ij}\\in F\\), \\(1\\le i\\le3\\), \\(1\\le j\\le r\\), and parametrize\n\\[\nz_i(t)=\\sum_{j=1}^r x_{ij}t_j,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb F_2^r.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero input has uniform image in \\(F^3\\), so noninjectivity has probability at most \\((2^r-1)q^{-3}\\). Conditioned on injectivity, the image is a uniform binary \\(r\\)-subspace. Distinct nonzero binary inputs are linearly independent over \\(F\\), so their images are independent uniform elements of \\(F^3\\). A union bound gives failure of nonproportionality with probability \\(O_r(q^{-2})\\), apart from noninjectivity.</p><p>Fix a set \\(E\\) of more than \\(3r\\) distinct nonzero inputs. Alignment on all of \\(E\\), for some binary linear \\(g\\), is equivalent to membership of\n\\[\nc=\\bigl(\\sqrt{z_1(t)z_2(t)z_3(t)}\\bigr)_{t\\in E}\n\\]\nin the \\(F\\)-column span of\n\\[\nM_{t,(i,j)}=t_jz_i(t).\n\\]\nThis membership accounts simultaneously for all choices of the basis values of \\(g\\).</p><p>Regard the \\(x_{ij}\\) as indeterminates and set \\(L=\\mathbb F_2(x_{ij})\\). The elements\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x_{1j}x_{2k}x_{3l}},\\qquad 1\\le j,k,l\\le r,\n\\]\nare linearly independent over \\(L\\): after clearing denominators, their distinct parity vectors prevent cancellation in a polynomial identity in the square roots of the indeterminates.</p><p>Expanding \\(c\\) in these elements gives coefficient vectors\n\\[\nv_{jkl}=(t_jt_kt_l)_{t\\in E}.\n\\]\nIf \\(c\\) belonged to the column span of \\(M\\) over an algebraic closure of \\(L\\), every \\(L\\)-row vector annihilating \\(M\\) would annihilate each \\(v_{jkl}\\). Double annihilation would put every \\(v_{jkl}\\) in \\(\\operatorname{col}_L(M)\\).</p><p>Let \\(V_h\\le L^E\\) be the evaluation span of positive-degree monomials in \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_r\\) of degree at most \\(h\\). Repeated indices in \\(v_{jkl}\\) give degrees one and two as well as three. Thus\n\\[\nV_3\\subseteq\\operatorname{col}_L(M)\\subseteq V_2\\subseteq V_3.\n\\]\nThe common space is preserved by multiplication by each \\(t_i\\), contains all positive-degree monomials, and therefore contains every point indicator on the nonzero set \\(E\\). Its dimension would be \\(|E|&gt;3r\\), contradicting the number of columns of \\(M\\).</p><p>Square the entries of \\(M,c\\). The augmented squared matrix has a nonzero minor of size \\(h+1\\), where \\(h=\\operatorname{rank}_L M\\le3r\\), of degree at most \\(2h+3\\le6r+3\\). Every unaugmented minor of that size vanishes identically. Consequently specialized column membership forces this nonzero polynomial to vanish. The polynomial zero bound [40, Corollary 1] bounds its probability by \\((6r+3)/q\\).</p><p>Failure of the alignment condition occurs on some set \\(E\\) of size \\(3r+1\\). There are finitely many such sets depending only on \\(r\\); if \\(2^r-1\\le3r\\), there are none. A union bound and conditioning on injectivity prove genericity.</p><p>Finally, Lemma 1.3 shows that rank loss requires a nonzero aligned character on the selected line. Genericity permits at most one such character per line and at most \\(3r\\) such lines. A loss of one changes the harmonic potential by \\(1/r\\), giving (3.9). Without genericity, at most \\(2^r-1\\) lines can cause loss, giving (3.10).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nChoose \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nH_{r_0}2^{r_0}\\epsilon_0\\le1,\\qquad \\epsilon_0&lt;1. \\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nUsing Lemma 1.4, fix \\(d\\) sufficiently large that \\(b=3d\\ge r_0\\), and a uniform rank-\\(r\\) subspace of \\(B^*\\) is nongeneric with probability at most \\(\\epsilon_0\\), for every \\(1\\le r\\le r_0\\). Fix one generic rank-\\(r_0\\) subspace.</p><p>Define recursively binary spaces \\(P_t\\), shift subspaces \\(R_t\\le P_t\\), surjections \\(\\lambda_t:R_t\\to B\\), and functions \\(C_t:P_t\\to B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At height zero, \\(P_0=R_0=B\\) and \\(C_0=\\lambda_0=\\operatorname{id}_B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal I\\) be all pairs \\((A,J)\\), where \\(A\\) is an \\(F\\)-line in \\(F^3\\) and \\(J:B\\to U_A\\) is a binary linear isomorphism.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nP_t=\\prod_{(A,J)\\in\\mathcal I}P_{t-1},\n\\qquad\n(x,y)=\\sum_{A,J}JC_{t-1}(u_{AJ}),\n\\qquad\nC_t(u)=y+Q(x).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nR_t=\\left\\{(h_{AJ})\\in\\prod_{A,J}R_{t-1}:\n\\sum_{A,J}J\\lambda_{t-1}(h_{AJ})\\in\\{0\\}\\oplus B\\right\\},\n\\]\nand define \\(\\lambda_t(h)=k\\) when the displayed aggregate is \\((0,k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\xi_0\\) be uniform on \\(B\\). For \\(t\\ge1\\), choose a uniform child and embed an independent copy of \\(\\xi_{t-1}\\) there, with zero in every other child.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The maps \\(\\lambda_t\\) are surjective and\n\\[\nC_t(u+h)=C_t(u)+\\lambda_t(h)\\qquad(h\\in R_t). \\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nFor independent uniform \\(u\\in P_t\\) and noise \\(\\xi_t\\),\n\\[\np_t:=\\Pr[C_t(u+\\xi_t)\\ne C_t(u)]\n=(1-q^{-3})(1-\\theta)^t. \\tag{3.15}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The shift identity follows directly from the aggregate definition. Surjectivity follows inductively from Lemma 1.1: child output translations can be chosen arbitrarily, and their aggregate map is onto \\(X\\oplus B\\). The shift identity and surjectivity also show that uniform input has uniform \\(C_t\\)-output.</p><p>The formula for \\(p_0\\) is immediate. At a higher level, condition on the selected child \\((A,J)\\), and write \\(\\Delta\\) for its output difference. Its law is independent of the selected index.</p><p>For a fixed inner perturbation \\(a\\), the shift identity gives\n\\[\nC_{t-1}(u+a+h)+C_{t-1}(u+h)\n=C_{t-1}(u+a)+C_{t-1}(u)\n\\qquad(h\\in R_{t-1}).\n\\]\nThus the difference is invariant under shifts that translate the initial output by every element of \\(B\\). On uniform input, that initial output is uniform conditional on the difference, even when the inner perturbation is specified. Other child outputs remain independently uniform.</p><p>The aggregate is therefore uniform conditional on \\((A,J,\\Delta)\\), and independent of \\(J\\Delta\\). Conditional on \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\) and \\(A\\), the isomorphism \\(J\\) remains uniform, so \\(J\\Delta\\) is uniform on \\(U_A\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Lemma 1.2 gives parent change probability \\(1-\\theta\\). If \\(\\Delta=0\\), the parent does not change. Hence \\(p_t=p_{t-1}(1-\\theta)\\), proving the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the recursive construction of Lemma 1.5.", "statement": "<p>Assume the recursive construction of Lemma 1.5. For \\(S\\le B^*\\), a lift on \\(P_t\\) is a binary linear map \\(z\\mapsto\\psi_z\\in P_t^*\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\psi_z|_{R_t}=z\\circ\\lambda_t. \\tag{3.16}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(t\\ge1\\), restriction of a lift to each child’s shift space factors through \\(\\lambda_{t-1}\\). Its induced characters on the child output space have the form\n\\[\n\\phi_{AJ}(z)=J^*((\\gamma_z,z)|_{U_A})\\in B^*, \\tag{3.17}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\gamma:S\\to X^*\\) is one binary linear map common to all children.</p><p>After choosing a child, putting \\(S'=\\operatorname{im}\\phi_{AJ}\\), and choosing a linear section \\(\\iota:S'\\to S\\), restriction of \\(\\psi_{\\iota(z')}\\) to that child input gives a lift on \\(P_{t-1}\\) with domain \\(S'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A shift supported on one child and lying in \\(\\ker\\lambda_{t-1}\\) is a parent shift of output translation zero. Every \\(\\psi_z\\) vanishes on it, proving factorization.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal T=\\prod_{A,J}B\\) be the space of child translations, with surjective aggregate map \\(\\mathcal A:\\mathcal T\\to X\\oplus B\\). The sum of induced child characters, minus \\(z\\) applied to the second component of \\(\\mathcal A\\), vanishes whenever the first component is zero. It therefore factors uniquely through that first component, onto \\(X\\); call the resulting character \\(\\gamma_z\\). Uniqueness makes \\(\\gamma_z\\) linear in \\(z\\). Evaluating a tuple supported on one child gives (3.17).</p><p>A section \\(\\iota\\) supplies the asserted child lift. Fix deterministic section rules depending only on the path already chosen, not on future child indices or the final noise symbol. Every surviving functional is consequently a restriction of a member of the original family.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "Proposition 3.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the construction and parameter choices of Lemmas 1.5–1.6.", "statement": "<p>Assume the construction and parameter choices of Lemmas 1.5–1.6. A lift is full if its domain is \\(B^*\\). Set\n\\[\ns=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{H_{r_0}}{8\\theta}\\right\\rfloor. \\tag{3.18}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every full lift on \\(P_s\\) detects \\(\\xi_s\\) with probability at least \\(1/4\\):\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\exists z\\in B^*:\\psi_z(\\xi_s)\\ne0]\\ge\\frac14. \\tag{3.19}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Restrict the top lift to the fixed generic rank-\\(r_0\\) subspace. Follow the random leaf path, replacing the lift at each child by the image lift of Lemma 1.6. Let \\(S_j\\) be the domain after \\(j\\) steps, and \\(r_j=\\dim S_j\\). Continue with zero after extinction.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) contain all path choices preceding the outgoing \\((A_j,J_j)\\). The current space, lift, and map \\(\\gamma_j\\) are \\(\\mathcal F_j\\)-measurable. Conditional on \\(\\mathcal F_j,A_j\\),\n\\[\nE_{j,A_j}=\\operatorname{im}\\bigl[z\\mapsto(\\gamma_{j,z},z)|_{U_{A_j}}\\bigr]\n\\]\nis fixed, and\n\\[\nS_{j+1}=J_j^*E_{j,A_j},\\qquad r_{j+1}=\\dim E_{j,A_j}.\n\\]\nThe rank is fixed before \\(J_j\\) is revealed. A uniform \\(J_j\\) makes \\(S_{j+1}\\) a uniform subspace of that rank. If \\(B_j\\) indicates positive rank and nongenericity, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[B_{j+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_j,A_j]\\le\\epsilon_0. \\tag{3.20}\n\\]\nGenericity bounds every extension map, including the next lift’s map even though that lift depends on \\(J_j\\).</p><p>For \\(\\Delta_j=H_{r_j}-H_{r_{j+1}}\\), Lemma 1.4 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\\Delta_j\\mid\\mathcal F_j]\n\\le3\\theta+H_{r_0}2^{r_0}\\theta B_j.\n\\]\nInitially the space is generic; subsequently average (3.20). Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\Delta_j\n\\le(3+H_{r_0}2^{r_0}\\epsilon_0)\\theta\\le4\\theta.\n\\]\nNo independence between an incoming orientation and its subsequent lift is assumed.</p><p>Summing,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[H_{r_0}-H_{r_s}]\\le4s\\theta\\le H_{r_0}/2.\n\\]\nExtinction requires a drop of \\(H_{r_0}\\), so \\(\\Pr[r_s=0]\\le1/2\\).</p><p>At a leaf, \\(P_0=R_0=B\\), and a surviving rank-\\(r_s\\) family detects an independent uniform symbol with probability \\(1-2^{-r_s}\\ge1/2\\). Each surviving functional restricts an original one. Multiplying the survival and detection bounds proves \\(1/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 4.1, Lemma 4.2, and equation (4.1)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nA weighted parity instance consists of a finite variable set, a nonempty set \\(\\mathcal E\\) of occurrence IDs, positive rational weights \\(\\omega_e\\) summing to one, and ordered equations\n\\[\nx_{v_{e,1}}+x_{v_{e,2}}+x_{v_{e,3}}=b_e,\\qquad b_e\\in\\mathbb F_2.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\) for its maximum satisfied weight.</p><p>Its equation–variable projection game \\(G_{\\mathcal E}\\) samples \\(e\\) according to \\(\\omega\\) and \\(j\\) uniformly from \\([3]\\). The equation prover receives \\(e\\) and returns a satisfying triple; the variable prover receives \\(v_{e,j}\\) and returns a bit. Acceptance is equality at position \\(j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed rational \\(0&lt;\\xi&lt;1/100\\), a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from Boolean satisfiability produces weighted parity instances with three distinct variable IDs in every occurrence, polynomial-bit rational weights, and\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\ge1-\\xi,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\le4/5.\n\\]\nOn the NO instances,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathcal E})\\le14/15.\n\\]\nThe answer alphabet sizes are four and two, and the question pair determines the projection constraint.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 2.1 with \\(\\xi&lt;1/100\\), then Lemma 2.2. Completeness is unchanged, while\n\\[\n\\frac{1+(1/2+\\xi)}2+\\frac3{100}&lt;\\frac45.\n\\]</p><p>For any variable-prover strategy, its bits define a global assignment. On an equation violated by that assignment, every satisfying equation answer disagrees in at least one position. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathcal E})\n\\le1-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)}3\n\\le\\frac{14}{15}.\n\\]\nDistinct positions ensure that an equation ID and a variable ID determine the chosen position and hence the projection constraint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Theorem 4.1 (Weighted parity gap); external hardness input", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Statement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed rational \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), there is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from Boolean satisfiability to weighted parity instances with binary-encoded rational weights such that\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\ge1-\\xi,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\le\\frac12+\\xi.\n\\]\nThe polynomial may depend on \\(\\xi\\). Distinct variable IDs within an occurrence are not assumed in this input statement.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the gap consequence of Håstad’s construction [18, Theorem 5.4 and its proof]. Its fixed dyadic verifier error \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) gives completeness \\(1-\\delta\\) and soundness \\((1+\\delta)/2\\). Enumerating verifier choices as equations with their rational probabilities gives a deterministic weighted reduction. Choose \\(\\delta\\le\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Making positions distinct)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume an arbitrary weighted parity instance \\(\\mathcal E\\), allowing repeated variable IDs within an equation.", "statement": "<p>Assume an arbitrary weighted parity instance \\(\\mathcal E\\), allowing repeated variable IDs within an equation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A deterministic polynomial-time construction gives \\(\\mathcal E'\\) with distinct variable IDs in each occurrence and positive rational weights of polynomial bit length, satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E')\\ge\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E')\n\\le\\frac{1+\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)}2+\\frac3{100}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace each variable \\(v\\) by one hundred clones \\((v,c)\\), \\(c\\in[100]\\). For each equation, choose its clones uniformly subject to distinct clones whenever positions have the same underlying variable. List all resulting occurrences with their conditional weights. There are at most \\(100^3\\) choices per occurrence. Assigning all clones the original variable’s bit proves completeness.</p><p>Fix arbitrary clone bits and set\n\\[\nt_v=\\frac1{100}\\sum_{c=1}^{100}(-1)^{x_{(v,c)}}.\n\\]\nBefore conditioning on distinct clones, an equation’s satisfaction probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{1+(-1)^{b_e}t_{v_{e,1}}t_{v_{e,2}}t_{v_{e,3}}}{2}.\n\\]\nRound each bias to a majority bit, resolving zeros arbitrarily. Every equation violated by the rounded assignment has nonpositive signed product and satisfaction probability at most \\(1/2\\); other equations have probability at most one. The weighted mean is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)}2.\n\\]\nThe probability of a forbidden clone collision is at most \\(3/100\\), by a union bound over position pairs. Conditioning on its complement changes a bounded expectation by at most \\(3/100\\). Conditional denominators have constant size apart from the original weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Sections 4.2–4.4, equations (4.4)–(4.6), and Lemma 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal E\\) is a weighted parity instance with distinct positions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(k\\ge1\\). An equation question \\(U=(e_1,\\ldots,e_k)\\) has distribution \\(\\omega^{\\otimes k}\\). Blocks use separate local answer copies even when IDs repeat.</p><p class=\"li\">An equation answer is represented by its first two bits \\(x_i,y_i\\), with third bit \\(b_{e_i}+x_i+y_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Add a common homogeneous coordinate \\(t_0\\). Thus \\(L_U=\\mathbb F_2^{1+2k}\\); actual answers are precisely the vectors with \\(e_0^\\top z=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A permitted projection replaces any chosen equation blocks by one of their three actual variable bits. A projected question \\(O\\) retains equation or variable IDs at their ordered block positions. Its homogeneous space \\(L_O\\) has two coordinates per retained equation and one per singleton, in addition to \\(t_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The projection extends to a surjection \\(\\bar\\pi:L_U\\to L_O\\) preserving \\(t_0\\); the three singleton coordinates are\n\\[\nx_i,\\qquad y_i,\\qquad b_{e_i}t_0+x_i+y_i.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix latent data \\(V\\supseteq K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell,C,\\mu\\) as in Proposition 1 and a splitting \\(V=K\\oplus W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A table on \\(Q\\) is \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(L_Q,V)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its reduced support retains a rank-two equation slope block, omits rank-zero blocks, and replaces rank-one pairs \\((v,0),(0,v),(v,v)\\) by the corresponding actual variable bit. The last replacement adds \\(b_ev\\) to the homogeneous intercept. A singleton is retained exactly when its slope is nonzero.</p><p class=\"li\">A key records the support’s positions, equation/variable tags and IDs, and the entire induced reduced affine table, including its intercept.</p><p class=\"li\">Vertices are orbits of keys under addition of constant \\(h\\in K\\). The representative has reduced intercept with zero \\(K\\)-component.</p><p class=\"li\">For a vertex labeling \\(\\lambda\\), let \\(c(Q,P)\\in K\\) be the removed \\(K\\)-component, \\(v(Q,P)\\) the orbit vertex, and\n\\[\nF_Q(P)=\\lambda(v(Q,P))+c(Q,P).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The construction is well-defined, and restored functions satisfy\n\\[\nF_Q(P+he_0^\\top)=F_Q(P)+h, \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\\[\nF_U(P'\\bar\\pi)=F_O(P') \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nfor every permitted projection and table \\(P'\\) on \\(O\\).</p><p>Define the weighted matrix game by independent samples\n\\[\nU\\sim\\omega^{\\otimes k},\\quad\nP\\text{ uniform in }\\operatorname{Hom}(L_U,V),\\quad\na\\sim\\mu,\\quad\nl\\text{ uniform in }\\mathbb F_2^{1+2k},\n\\]\nand test\n\\[\nF_U(P)=F_U(P+al^\\top). \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nEach sample is a translation constraint on \\(K\\), with its sampling probability as weight; loops and repeated occurrences are allowed.</p><p>If \\(\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\ge1-\\xi\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\ge1-k\\xi-p_*/2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a table on \\(Q\\), let \\(J_3\\) be its rank-one equation blocks reduced to their third bit, with slopes \\(v_i\\). If the original intercept is \\(c\\), the reduced intercept is\n\\[\n\\widetilde c=c+\\sum_{i\\in J_3}b_{e_i}v_i. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nThe support projection \\(\\rho_{Q,P}:L_Q\\to L_{\\mathrm{supp}}\\) retains \\(t_0\\), uses the specified actual-bit coordinates, and discards omitted blocks. It is surjective by independent block choices. The block identities give\n\\[\nP=\\widetilde P\\rho_{Q,P},\n\\]\nand surjectivity makes \\(\\widetilde P\\) unique.</p><p>A projected table and its pullback have the same key. Retained equations reduce identically. A zero singleton disappears on both sides. A nonzero singleton pulls back to its corresponding rank-one pattern and reduces to the same variable ID at the same position. For a third-position singleton, pullback adds \\(b_ev\\) to the intercept and reduction adds it again; the terms cancel. All other corrections agree. Thus projected keys already belong to the universe of keys from equation questions.</p><p>Adding \\(he_0^\\top\\) changes only the reduced intercept. The \\(K\\)-action is free because the key records that intercept, and the chosen representative is unique. This proves folding (4.4). Equality of exact keys proves sharing (4.5).</p><p>If the restored offsets at the two test endpoints are \\(c_0,c_1\\), equality is the permutation constraint\n\\[\n\\lambda(v_1)=\\lambda(v_0)+c_0+c_1.\n\\]\nThus every edge is a translation.</p><p>For completeness, fix a global parity assignment of value at least \\(1-\\xi\\). On every reduced support, use its global singleton bits; on a retained equation, use the first two global bits and set the third to satisfy the equation. These choices depend only on the support. Label an orbit representative by applying \\(C\\) to its reduced table evaluated at this answer. Equivariance of \\(C\\) makes the restored answer evaluation of the actual reduced table followed by \\(C\\).</p><p>With probability at least \\(1-k\\xi\\), every equation of \\(U\\) is globally satisfied. Then all support answers are projections of one actual answer \\(z_U\\in L_U\\). The tested values are\n\\[\nC(Pz_U),\\qquad C(Pz_U+a(l^\\top z_U)).\n\\]\nSince \\(z_U\\ne0\\), \\(Pz_U\\) is uniform on \\(V\\), independent of \\(a,l\\), and \\(l^\\top z_U\\) is an independent fair bit. The failure probability on these tuples is at most \\(p_*/2\\). Add the probability of an unsatisfied tuple.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 5.1 (Equality soundness for degree-two shortcode)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nLet\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Mat}_{\\ell,m}=\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb F_2^m,\\mathbb F_2^\\ell).\n\\]\nA row and column slice is a nonempty set\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{M:d_i^\\top M=s_i^\\top\\ (1\\le i\\le n_r),\\\nMq_j=t_j\\ (1\\le j\\le n_c)\\}, \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_i,t_j\\in\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) and \\(s_i,q_j\\in\\mathbb F_2^m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\eta\\in(0,1)\\), there exist \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1]\\), an integer \\(r_s\\ge1\\), and an integer \\(\\ell_0\\), depending only on \\(\\eta\\), such that for every \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\) there is \\(m_0(\\eta,\\ell)\\) with the following property.</p><p>If \\(m\\ge m_0(\\eta,\\ell)\\) and \\(f:\\operatorname{Mat}_{\\ell,m}\\to\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr_{M,a,l}[f(M)=f(M+al^\\top)]\\ge\\eta, \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(M,a,l\\) are independent uniform samples, then some slice \\(\\mathcal S\\) with \\(n_r,n_c\\le r_s\\) and some \\(z\\in\\mathbb F_2^m,u\\in\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\Pr_{M\\mid\\mathcal S}[f(M)=Mz+u]\\ge\\alpha. \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nNo folding assumption is imposed.</p><p>For rational \\(\\eta\\), these constants and dimension thresholds can be chosen effectively, with rational \\(\\alpha\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the Grassmann expansion theorem [25, Theorem 1.12, 2018 revision], with the matrix chart of [6, Definition 3.2 and Lemmas 3.3–3.7]. That theorem says that a nonempty set of Grassmann vertices of retention at least \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) has positive relative density in an interval\n\\[\n\\{L:A_0\\subseteq L\\subseteq B_0\\},\n\\qquad\n\\dim A_0+\\operatorname{codim}_E B_0\\le r,\n\\]\nwith density and \\(r\\) depending only on \\(\\zeta\\), for sufficiently large vertex dimension and then ambient dimension.</p><p>Equality acceptance averages the retentions of the nonempty fibers \\(f^{-1}(y)\\), weighted by their masses. Some fiber \\(S\\) has retention at least \\(\\eta\\). The probability that a perturbation factor is zero is\n\\[\np_0=2^{-\\ell}+2^{-m}-2^{-\\ell-m}.\n\\]\nChoose thresholds so that \\(p_0\\le\\eta/2\\). Conditional on both factors being nonzero, \\(S\\) has retention at least \\((\\eta-p_0)/(1-p_0)\\ge\\eta/2\\).</p><p>Associate to \\(M\\) the subspace\n\\[\nL_M=\\{(x,M^\\top x):x\\in\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\}\n\\le E=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\oplus\\mathbb F_2^m.\n\\]\nThis parametrizes the chart \\(\\mathcal C\\) whose first projection is invertible, and\n\\[\n\\dim(L_M\\cap L_N)=\\ell-\\operatorname{rank}(M-N).\n\\]\nWithin-chart neighbors are exactly nonzero rank-one differences. Each chart vertex has \\((2^\\ell-1)(2^m-1)\\) chart neighbors and full Grassmann degree\n\\[\n(2^\\ell-1)(2^{m+1}-2).\n\\]\nExactly half its neighbors lie in the chart. Over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), every nonzero rank-one matrix has a unique factorization with nonzero factors. Thus \\(\\widetilde S=\\{L_M:M\\in S\\}\\) has Grassmann retention at least \\(\\eta/4\\).</p><p>Apply expansion with \\(\\zeta=\\eta/4\\), obtaining density \\(\\alpha\\) on an interval of complexity at most \\(r_s\\). Intersecting the interval with \\(\\mathcal C\\) only increases the relative density of \\(\\widetilde S\\), since \\(\\widetilde S\\subseteq\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>A basis \\((d_i,s_i)\\) of \\(A_0\\) gives \\(d_i^\\top M=s_i^\\top\\). A basis \\((t_j,q_j)\\) of \\(B_0^\\perp\\) gives \\(Mq_j=t_j\\). The intersection is therefore a nonempty slice with at most \\(r_s\\) specifications of each kind. On at least an \\(\\alpha\\) fraction, \\(f\\) is the value defining \\(S\\). Take \\(z=0\\) and \\(u\\) equal to that value.</p><p>The constants depend only on \\(\\eta\\), with the remaining ambient threshold absorbed into \\(m_0(\\eta,\\ell)\\). Effectiveness follows from [25, Theorem 2.13 and Section 2.4], together with [25, Lemmas 2.7–2.8].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.5 (Good row advice and its short answer set)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Parameter order and assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Parameter order and assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(p_*&gt;0\\) and its \\(r_*\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(\\eta=2^{-r_*}/16\\), and choose \\(\\alpha,r_s\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\ell\\) sufficiently large for Propositions 1 and 4 and\n\\[\n2^{-(\\ell-r_s)}&lt;\\alpha/8,\\qquad 2^{-\\ell}&lt;\\eta/4. \\tag{5.4}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the latent data at this \\(\\ell\\), and \\(V=K\\oplus W\\), \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\), before \\(k\\) grows.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m=1+2k\\). Tables on \\(U\\) are written \\((M,T)\\), with \\(M:L_U\\to K\\), \\(T:L_U\\to W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let functions \\(F_Q:\\operatorname{Hom}(L_Q,V)\\to K\\) satisfy folding and sharing as in Proposition 3, and suppose their latent matrix-test acceptance is at least \\(0.99\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample independently \\(U\\sim\\omega^{\\otimes k}\\), uniform \\(T\\), uniform \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^{r_s}\\), and uniform \\(M:L_U\\to K\\).</p><p>A row advice \\((U,T,A,S_0)\\) is good if some at most \\(r_s\\) column specifications \\(Mq_j=t_j\\) and some target \\(Mz+u\\) give a nonempty slice inside \\(AM=S_0\\) with agreement at least \\(\\alpha/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[(U,T,A,AM)\\text{ is good}]\\ge g_0,\n\\qquad\ng_0=10\\eta^2\\,2^{-\\ell r_s}&gt;0. \\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThe threshold is independent of the parity instance and the function family.</p><p>For every positive \\(k\\), each good row advice admits a deterministic choice of such a witness with \\(e_0^\\top z=1\\). Writing \\(Z=\\operatorname{span}\\{q_j\\}\\), it gives a short nonempty set of actual answers\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=(z+Z)\\cap\\{v\\in L_U:e_0^\\top v=1\\},\n\\qquad\n1\\le|\\mathcal A|\\le2^{r_s}. \\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nThe witness depends only on the visible row advice and the fixed family, and retains the agreement guarantee.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\((U,T)\\) of surrogate acceptance at least \\(4\\eta\\), and write \\(f(M)=F_U(M,T)\\). Let \\(B\\) be the union, over all \\(A,S_0\\), of the good row fibers. Suppose \\(\\Pr[B]&lt;\\eta\\). Replace \\(f\\) on \\(B\\) by independent uniform values of \\(K\\), assigning one replacement to each matrix even if fibers overlap. Both endpoints of the shortcode step are uniform, so every replacement realization preserves acceptance at least \\(2\\eta\\).</p><p>Put \\(r=r_s\\). A nonempty slice with at most \\(r\\) row and \\(r\\) column specifications has homogeneous space\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}\\!\\left(\n\\mathbb F_2^m/\\operatorname{span}\\{q_j\\},\n\\bigcap_i\\ker d_i^\\top\n\\right)\n\\]\nand size \\(n_{\\mathcal S}\\ge2^{(\\ell-r)(m-r)}\\). Padding shorter systems with zero equations, there are at most\n\\[\n2^{(2r+1)(\\ell+m)}\n\\]\nslice–target descriptions.</p><p>For one description, count only randomized-entry matches. They are independent bounded variables with mean at most\n\\[\n2^{-\\ell}n_{\\mathcal S}&lt;\\alpha n_{\\mathcal S}/8.\n\\]\nHoeffding’s inequality bounds the probability of at least \\(\\alpha n_{\\mathcal S}/4\\) such matches by\n\\[\n\\exp(-\\alpha^2n_{\\mathcal S}/32).\n\\]\nThe union bound is at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left(\n(2r+1)(\\ell+m)\\log2\n-\\frac{\\alpha^2}{32}2^{(\\ell-r)(m-r)}\n\\right),\n\\]\nwhich tends to zero. Fix replacements contributing less than \\(\\alpha/4\\) on every slice–target description.</p><p>If a slice’s row fiber was good, its entries all lie in \\(B\\), so modified agreement is less than \\(\\alpha/4\\). Otherwise original agreement is less than \\(\\alpha/2\\), and randomized matches add less than \\(\\alpha/4\\); modified agreement is less than \\(3\\alpha/4\\). These cases cover every slice in Proposition 4, contradicting that proposition applied to the modified function of acceptance at least \\(2\\eta\\). Folding need not survive the modification.</p><p>Thus \\(\\Pr[B]\\ge\\eta\\). For each \\(M\\in B\\), some \\(A\\) has a good fiber through \\(M\\), and uniform \\(A\\) hits that matrix with probability \\(2^{-\\ell r_s}\\). Lemma 5.1 supplies at least \\(10\\eta\\) of the pairs \\((U,T)\\), proving (5.8).</p><p>For the answer-set assertion, fix a good witness. Nonemptiness makes the prescribed column values a linear map \\(t:Z\\to K\\). For \\(w\\in Z\\), replacing\n\\[\nz\\mapsto z+w,\\qquad u\\mapsto u+t(w)\n\\]\npreserves the target on the slice.</p><p>If \\(z+Z\\) had no vector of first bit one, \\(z\\) and every vector in \\(Z\\) would have first bit zero. For \\(H=\\ker A\\), every translation \\(M\\mapsto M+he_0^\\top\\), \\(h\\in H\\), would preserve the slice and target, while folding translates \\(F_U\\) by \\(h\\). At most one matrix per free \\(H\\)-orbit could match. Agreement would be at most\n\\[\n|H|^{-1}=2^{-\\ell+\\operatorname{rank}A}\n\\le2^{-(\\ell-r_s)}&lt;\\alpha/8,\n\\]\ncontradicting goodness.</p><p>Choose a first-bit-one representative in the coset and a witness by fixed finite orders. Then \\(z\\in\\mathcal A\\), and \\(|\\mathcal A|\\le2^{\\dim Z}\\le2^{r_s}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.4 (Spectral comparison)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the parameter choices, table family, and acceptance hypothesis of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>Assume the parameter choices, table family, and acceptance hypothesis of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For independent uniform \\(M,a,l\\), with \\(a\\in K\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U,T}\\Pr_{M,a,l}\n[F_U(M,T)=F_U(M+al^\\top,T)]\n\\ge0.9\\,2^{-r_*}. \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nConsequently at least \\(10\\eta\\) of the pairs \\((U,T)\\) have inner acceptance at least \\(4\\eta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\sigma\\in K^*\\), let \\(f_{U,\\sigma}(P)=(-1)^{\\sigma(F_U(P))}\\). A matrix frequency is \\(g=(g_i)_{i=0}^{m-1}\\in(V^*)^m\\), with character\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\sum_i g_i(Pe_i)}.\n\\]\nUse normalized Fourier coefficients. Translation by \\(al^\\top\\), averaged over uniform \\(l\\), has multiplier\n\\[\n\\Lambda_g=\\Pr_{a\\sim\\mu}[g_i(a)=0\\text{ for every }i].\n\\]\nIt lies in \\([0,1]\\), and Proposition 1 bounds it by \\(7/8\\) when\n\\[\n\\dim\\operatorname{span}\\{g_i|_K\\}\\ge r_*.\n\\]</p><p>Expanding equality using a uniform output character and Fourier orthogonality gives acceptance\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U,\\sigma}\\sum_g|\\widehat f_{U,\\sigma}(g)|^2\\Lambda_g.\n\\]\nParseval gives total mass one. If \\(B\\) is average mass at restricted ranks at least \\(r_*\\), then\n\\[\n0.99\\le1-B/8,\\qquad B\\le0.08.\n\\]\nAt least \\(0.9\\) of the average mass therefore lies below \\(r_*\\).</p><p>For uniform \\(a\\in K\\), the multiplier is\n\\[\n2^{-\\dim\\operatorname{span}\\{g_i|_K\\}}.\n\\]\nThis gives (5.7), and the perturbation fixes \\(T\\). Its average is at least \\(14.4\\eta\\). If \\(q\\) is the proportion with inner acceptance at least \\(4\\eta\\), the average is at most \\(4\\eta+q\\), so \\(q\\ge10.4\\eta\\ge10\\eta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.3 (Matrix decoding), with Definition 5.2 (Projected advice experiment)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.\nUse the fixed latent and shortcode parameters, in the order specified in Proposition 5. For each equation question and permitted projected question \\(Q\\), let\n\\[\nF_Q:\\operatorname{Hom}(L_Q,V)\\to K\n\\]\nsatisfy\n\\[\nF_Q(P+ce_0^\\top)=F_Q(P)+c,\n\\qquad\nF_U(P'\\bar\\pi)=F_O(P'). \\tag{5.6}\n\\]</p><p>Projected advice experiment.</p><p class=\"li\">Sample \\(U\\sim\\omega^{\\otimes k}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Independently in each block, retain the equation with probability \\(1-\\beta\\), or retain a uniform one of its three variables with probability \\(\\beta\\). Let \\(O\\) be the resulting question and \\(\\bar\\pi:L_U\\to L_O\\) its projection.</p><p class=\"li\">Independently sample uniform maps\n\\[\nA:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^{r_s},\\quad\nT_O:L_O\\to W,\\quad\nM_O:L_O\\to K.\n\\]\nSet \\(S_O=AM_O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Send\n\\[\n(U,A,T_O\\bar\\pi,S_O\\bar\\pi)\n\\]\nto the \\(U\\)-prover and\n\\[\n(O,A,T_O,S_O)\n\\]\nto the \\(O\\)-prover.</p><p class=\"li\">Each prover returns an actual answer, of homogeneous first bit one. Success is \\(\\bar\\pi a_U=a_O\\). The matrix \\(M_O\\) beyond \\(S_O\\), and the projection itself, are not sent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) and \\(k_0\\ge8\\), depending only on the fixed parameters, such that for every cube \\(k\\ge k_0\\), setting \\(\\beta=k^{-2/3}\\), latent matrix-test acceptance at least \\(0.99\\) implies the existence of local strategies with full agreement probability at least \\(\\gamma\\). The bound is independent of the parity instance and of \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the two rules of Lemma 6.3. Lemma 6.2 supplies joint data of probability at least \\(\\gamma_1\\); conditional on those data, Lemma 6.3 gives agreement at least its stated bound. Thus take\n\\[\n\\gamma=\\gamma_1\\,2^{-\\ell r_s}(\\alpha/8)^2\\,2^{-r_s}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The largeness conditions on \\(k\\) come only from Proposition 4’s matrix dimension threshold, Proposition 5’s concentration estimate, and Lemma 6.1’s total variation estimate. They are uniform in the parity instance and function family.</p><p>Witness choices and Fourier sampling need not be efficient: they define strategies for a finite game. Averaging over independent private random tapes also produces deterministic strategies with at least the same agreement probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.6 (The padded matrix is asymptotically uniform)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the advice experiment of Proposition 6, with \\(k>1\\) an integer cube and \\(\\beta=k^{-2/3}\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume the advice experiment of Proposition 6, with \\(k&gt;1\\) an integer cube and \\(\\beta=k^{-2/3}\\). Average over the projection choices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Conditional on every \\(U\\), the law of\n\\[\n(M,T)=(M_O\\bar\\pi,T_O\\bar\\pi)\n\\]\nhas total variation distance \\(o(1)\\) from unrestricted uniform measure on\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(L_U,K)\\times\\operatorname{Hom}(L_U,W).\n\\]\nThe bound is uniform in \\(U\\) and the parity instance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Combine the components into a \\(V\\)-valued table and let \\(n=|V|\\). An unprojected block has independent uniform slopes; call this law \\(P_0\\). A singleton’s padded slopes have law \\(Q_0\\), the equal mixture of\n\\[\n(a,0),\\quad(0,a),\\quad(a,a),\\qquad a\\text{ uniform in }V.\n\\]\nThe likelihood ratio \\(dQ_0/dP_0\\) is \\(n\\) at \\((0,0)\\), \\(n/3\\) at the other \\(3(n-1)\\) points on these lines, and zero elsewhere. Hence\n\\[\n\\chi^2(Q_0\\|P_0)=\\frac{n-1}{3}.\n\\]\nThe block mixture \\((1-\\beta)P_0+\\beta Q_0\\) has divergence \\(\\beta^2(n-1)/3\\). Independence across blocks gives total divergence\n\\[\n\\left(1+\\frac{n-1}{3}\\beta^2\\right)^k-1=o(1), \\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nbecause \\(n\\) is fixed and \\(k\\beta^2=k^{-1/3}\\to0\\). Total variation is at most half its square root.</p><p>Third-bit projections add terms \\(b_ea\\) to the intercept. The original intercept is independent uniform on \\(V\\); adding their sum leaves it uniform and independent even conditional on the projection choices and slopes. Thus the bound covers the complete affine table.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.7 (Transfer of a chosen witness)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume Proposition 6’s hypotheses and matrix-test acceptance at least \\(0.99\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume Proposition 6’s hypotheses and matrix-test acceptance at least \\(0.99\\). For every good padded \\(U\\)-advice, choose the witness and answer set of Proposition 5. Write its column specifications as \\(Mq_j=t_j\\), target as \\(Mz+u\\), and \\(Z=\\operatorname{span}\\{q_j\\}\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nD=(U,O,\\pi,A,T_O,S_O)\n\\]\ndenote the joint data specifying both messages and the projection, but not \\(M_O\\) beyond \\(AM_O=S_O\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large cubes \\(k\\), a set of joint data of probability at least\n\\[\n\\gamma_1=\\frac{\\alpha g_0}{8}\\,2^{-\\ell r_s}&gt;0 \\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nhas good padded \\(U\\)-advice and a transferred nonempty slice\n\\[\nAM_O=S_O,\\qquad M_O\\bar\\pi q_j=t_j,\n\\]\non which, writing \\(z'=\\bar\\pi z\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[\nF_O(M_O,T_O)=M_Oz'+u\n\\mid AM_O=S_O,\\ M_O\\bar\\pi q_j=t_j\\ \\forall j\n]\\ge\\alpha/4. \\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nThe conditional matrix is uniform on that slice.</p>", "proof": "<p>First use unrestricted uniform \\(U,A,M,T\\), with \\(S_0=AM\\). Let \\(\\mathcal J\\) be goodness together with satisfaction of the chosen column specifications; let \\(\\mathcal M\\) be equality to the chosen target, false off good data.</p><p>Within a good row fiber, a nonempty column slice imposes at most \\(\\ell r_s\\) additional scalar equations. Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\mathcal J]\\ge g_0\\,2^{-\\ell r_s},\n\\qquad\n\\Pr[\\mathcal J\\cap\\mathcal M]\\ge(\\alpha/2)\\Pr[\\mathcal J].\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\mathcal J\\cap\\mathcal M]\n-\\frac\\alpha4\\Pr[\\mathcal J]\n\\ge\\frac{\\alpha g_0}{4}\\,2^{-\\ell r_s}. \\tag{5.14}\n\\]</p><p>These events depend only on \\((U,A,M,T)\\). Adding independent \\(A\\) preserves Lemma 6.1’s variation bound. If that bound is \\(v_k=o(1)\\), the displayed difference changes by at most \\((1+\\alpha/4)v_k\\). For large \\(k\\), its padded value is at least \\(\\gamma_1\\).</p><p>Conditional on \\(D\\), \\(M_O\\) is uniform on its row fiber, and the chosen witness is fixed. Let \\(p_D\\) be the conditional probability of the projected column slice, zero on nongood data. When \\(p_D&gt;0\\), let \\(q_D\\) be its equality fraction; otherwise set \\(q_D=0\\). Sharing identifies the padded difference as\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D[p_D(q_D-\\alpha/4)]\\ge\\gamma_1.\n\\]\nThe integrand is nonpositive outside goodness, \\(p_D&gt;0\\), \\(q_D\\ge\\alpha/4\\), and is at most one everywhere. That event has probability at least \\(\\gamma_1\\), proving the statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.8 (A transferred slice gives agreement)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume fixed joint data satisfying Lemma 6.2.", "statement": "<p>Assume fixed joint data satisfying Lemma 6.2.</p><p>Define the two local rules.</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(U\\)-prover samples uniformly from its chosen\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=(z+Z)\\cap\\{v:e_0^\\top v=1\\};\n\\]\non nongood inputs it returns any actual answer.</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(O\\)-prover puts \\(H=\\ker A\\), chooses a fixed representative \\(M_0\\) with \\(AM_0=S_O\\), and writes its row fiber as \\(M_0+\\mathcal X\\), where\n\\[\n\\mathcal X=\\operatorname{Hom}(L_O,H).\n\\]\nIt samples uniform \\(\\sigma\\in K^*\\), forms\n\\[\nf_\\sigma(N)=(-1)^{\\sigma(F_O(M_0+N,T_O))},\n\\]\nand samples \\(\\Phi\\in\\mathcal X^*\\) with probability \\(|\\widehat f_\\sigma(\\Phi)|^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(\\mathcal X^*=H^*\\otimes L_O\\) by\n\\[\n\\langle\\phi\\otimes v,N\\rangle=\\phi(Nv).\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\in H\\), write \\(\\Phi(h)\\in L_O\\) for contraction.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\sigma|_H\\ne0\\), choose by a fixed rule \\(h\\in H\\) with \\(\\sigma(h)=1\\), and return \\(\\Phi(h)\\). Otherwise return any actual answer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rules return actual answers, and with independent private randomness their conditional agreement probability is at least\n\\[\n2^{-\\ell r_s}(\\alpha/8)^2\\,2^{-r_s}. \\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nThe \\(O\\)-rule uses only its own advice, not the transferred witness.</p>", "proof": "<p>Folding gives\n\\[\nf_\\sigma(N+he_0^\\top)=-f_\\sigma(N)\n\\quad\\text{when }\\sigma(h)=1.\n\\]\nA nonzero Fourier coefficient therefore requires\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\langle\\Phi,he_0^\\top\\rangle}=-1,\n\\quad\\text{equivalently }e_0^\\top\\Phi(h)=1.\n\\]\nThus positive-mass frequencies give actual answers.</p><p>Put \\(Z'=\\bar\\pi Z\\). Since \\(\\bar\\pi\\) preserves the first coordinate,\n\\[\n\\bar\\pi\\mathcal A\n=(z'+Z')\\cap\\{v\\in L_O:e_0^\\top v=1\\}. \\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nThe projected column conditions fix \\(N|_{Z'}=b\\), for a consistent linear \\(b:Z'\\to H\\). Choose \\(N_*\\) in this affine slice. Define\n\\[\nC_\\sigma=\n\\mathbb E[\nf_\\sigma(N)(-1)^{\\sigma(Nz')}\n\\mid N|_{Z'}=b].\n\\]\nCharacter expansion of (5.13) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\sigma[(-1)^{\\sigma(M_0z'+u)}C_\\sigma]\\ge\\alpha/4,\n\\]\nso \\(\\mathbb E_\\sigma|C_\\sigma|\\ge\\alpha/4\\).</p><p>The fraction of \\(\\sigma\\) trivial on \\(H\\) is \\(2^{-\\dim H}\\le2^{-(\\ell-r_s)}&lt;\\alpha/8\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\sigma[\n1_{\\{\\sigma|_H\\ne0\\}}|C_\\sigma|\n]\\ge\\alpha/8. \\tag{5.16}\n\\]</p><p>The annihilator of maps vanishing on \\(Z'\\) is \\(H^*\\otimes Z'\\). With\n\\[\n\\theta_\\sigma=(\\sigma|_H)\\otimes z',\n\\]\nFourier expansion on the affine slice yields\n\\[\nC_\\sigma=\n\\sum_{\\Phi\\in\\theta_\\sigma+H^*\\otimes Z'}\n\\widehat f_\\sigma(\\Phi)\n(-1)^{\\langle\\Phi+\\theta_\\sigma,N_*\\rangle}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(2^{\\dim H\\dim Z'}\\le2^{\\ell r_s}\\) terms. Cauchy–Schwarz on the sum and then on the average gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\n1_{\\{\\sigma|_H\\ne0\\}}\n\\sum_{\\Phi\\in\\theta_\\sigma+H^*\\otimes Z'}\n|\\widehat f_\\sigma(\\Phi)|^2\n\\right]\n\\ge2^{-\\ell r_s}(\\alpha/8)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Every such sampled frequency is\n\\[\n\\Phi=(\\sigma|_H)\\otimes z'+\\Psi,\\qquad \\Psi\\in H^*\\otimes Z',\n\\]\nand consequently\n\\[\n\\Phi(h)=z'+\\Psi(h)\\in z'+Z'.\n\\]\nIts first bit is one, so it lies in \\(\\bar\\pi\\mathcal A\\). Independently, uniform \\(a_U\\in\\mathcal A\\) chooses a preimage with probability at least \\(1/|\\mathcal A|\\ge2^{-r_s}\\). Multiplication proves (5.15).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 6.1 (Projection-game repetition); external input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a finite bipartite projection game, with arbitrary probability distribution on constraint occurrences, arbitrary finite question and answer sets, and possibly parallel occurrences.</p><p class=\"li\">Each strategy uses only its own question.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G^{\\otimes n}\\) samples \\(n\\) independent occurrences, sends ordered question tuples, and accepts if all coordinate projections hold. Answers may depend on the entire local tuple.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(n\\ge1\\) and \\(0&lt;g&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\le1-g\n\\implies\n\\operatorname{val}(G^{\\otimes n})\n\\le(1-g^2/16)^n. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nThe bound is independent of question-set and alphabet sizes. Private or shared randomness independent of questions does not increase classical value.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Dinur–Steurer [13, Corollary 1.2]. Their Section 2.1 permits nonnegative edge weights and parallel edges; Section 2.2 uses the independent product stated here. Interchange the provers if necessary to match projection direction.</p><p>Randomness independent of the questions gives a mixture of deterministic strategies: sample complete answer tables before receiving the questions and average success probabilities. It cannot increase value.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 6.2 (Clean-coordinate bound)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal E\\) is a weighted parity instance with distinct positions and \\(\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\le4/5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix binary spaces \\(K,W\\), an integer \\(r_s\\ge1\\), and a cube \\(k&gt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the projected advice experiment defined in Proposition 6, with \\(\\beta=k^{-2/3}\\), independent uniform \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^{r_s}\\), \\(T_O:L_O\\to W\\), and \\(M_O:L_O\\to K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No high-acceptance or table-function assumption is imposed here.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every pair of local randomized strategies satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\bar\\pi a_U=a_O]\n\\le\n\\left(1-\\frac{\\beta\\,2^{-\\dim W-r_s}}{3600}\\right)^k\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2^{-\\dim W-r_s}}{3600}k^{1/3}\\right). \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nFor fixed advice spaces this tends to zero uniformly over the parity instance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on \\(A\\), and combine visible advice into\n\\[\nD_O=(T_O,S_O):L_O\\to E_A,\\qquad E_A=W\\oplus\\operatorname{im}A.\n\\]\nConditional on questions and projection choices, the original intercept \\(c_O\\) and all slope columns are independent uniform elements of \\(E_A\\). This law depends only on whether a block is an equation or singleton, not on IDs or selected positions.</p><p>A coordinate is clean if it is a singleton and its advice slope \\(d_i\\) is zero. Conditional on \\(A\\), clean indicators are independent with probability\n\\[\np_A=\\beta/|E_A|\n=\\beta\\,2^{-\\dim W-\\operatorname{rank}A}. \\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nMore precisely,\n\\[\n\\Pr[e_i=e,\\ i\\text{ clean at position }j\\mid A]\n=\\omega_e\\frac{\\beta}{3|E_A|}. \\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nThus conditional on cleanliness, each question pair has the ordinary equation–variable law.</p><p>Let \\(I\\) be the clean set. Condition on data \\(R\\) consisting of \\(A,I,c_O\\), and, for each \\(i\\notin I\\), its equation occurrence, retention/projection decision, selected position if projected, and advice slopes. Do not condition on witnesses, responses, agreement, or response randomness.</p><p>For every positive-probability \\(R=r\\), the clean pairs \\((e_i,v_{e_i,j_i})\\), \\(i\\in I\\), are independent question pairs of \\(G_{\\mathcal E}\\). Independence follows coordinatewise from (6.5); the outside data and independent intercept impose no further condition.</p><p>Each side can reconstruct its original local input from its own clean questions and the fixed \\(r\\). Pulling back a singleton slope \\(d_i\\) gives slopes\n\\[\n(d_i,0),\\quad(0,d_i),\\quad(d_i,d_i)\n\\]\nat positions one, two, and three, and the padded intercept is\n\\[\nc_U=c_O+\n\\sum_{\\substack{i\\text{ projected}\\\\j_i=3}}b_{e_i}d_i. \\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nAll clean slopes and clean intercept contributions vanish. Therefore the equation side reconstructs \\(U\\) and its padded advice without knowing clean selected positions or variable questions. The variable side reconstructs \\(O\\) and its advice without knowing clean equations. Outside coefficients and intercept corrections are fixed by \\(r\\). Repeated IDs cause no restriction because sampling is independent and local answer copies are separate.</p><p>Apply the original response rules to these reconstructed inputs and retain the clean-slot answers. This defines strategies for \\(G_{\\mathcal E}^{\\otimes|I|}\\). Full advice agreement implies acceptance in every clean slot.</p><p>Proposition 2 gives base value at most \\(14/15\\). Proposition 7 therefore gives, also interpreting \\(|I|=0\\) as bound one,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\bar\\pi a_U=a_O\\mid R=r]\n\\le(1-1/3600)^{|I|}.\n\\]\nConditional on \\(A\\), \\(|I|\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(k,p_A)\\). Average:\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\bar\\pi a_U=a_O]\n\\le\\mathbb E_A(1-p_A/3600)^k\n\\le\n\\left(1-\\frac{\\beta\\,2^{-\\dim W-r_s}}{3600}\\right)^k.\n\\]\nUse \\(\\operatorname{rank}A\\le r_s\\), \\(1-x\\le e^{-x}\\), and \\(k\\beta=k^{1/3}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 6.3 (A fixed gap with adjustable completeness)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.\nFix \\(p_*&gt;0\\), choose \\(r_*\\), then \\(\\eta=2^{-r_*}/16\\), then \\(\\alpha,r_s\\), then sufficiently large \\(\\ell\\) satisfying (5.4), and finally latent data \\(V=K\\oplus W,C,\\mu\\), exactly as in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large cube \\(k\\), the weighted translation matrix game \\(G\\) of Proposition 3, constructed from a parity instance \\(\\mathcal E\\) with distinct positions, satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\ge1-\\xi\n\\implies\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\ge1-k\\xi-p_*/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}(\\mathcal E)\\le4/5\n\\implies\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\le99/100.\n\\]\nThe lower threshold on \\(k\\) depends only on the fixed latent and shortcode data, not on \\(\\mathcal E\\) or \\(\\xi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Completeness is Proposition 3.</p><p>Fix a labeling on a NO parity instance. Its restored functions fold and share by Proposition 3. If matrix-test acceptance were at least \\(0.99\\), Proposition 6 would give advice strategies with agreement at least a fixed \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\). For all sufficiently large cubes \\(k\\), Proposition 8 bounds every such strategy pair by a quantity smaller than \\(\\gamma\\), a contradiction.</p><p>All thresholds are uniform in the parity instance. The parameter \\(p_*\\) can be arbitrarily small before fixing gadget data; after fixing \\(k\\), \\(\\xi\\) can be arbitrarily small. Hence the same NO gap permits any prescribed small completeness error.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 7.1 (Rounding and subdivision)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.\nLet \\(H\\) be a finite permutation-constraint game with \\(h\\ge1\\) positive rational occurrence weights summing to one. Loops and repeated occurrences are permitted. Fix positive rational \\(\\tau\\), and put \\(Q=\\lceil h/\\tau\\rceil\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic construction of an unweighted simple bipartite game \\(B\\) with \\(4Q\\) edges and the same alphabet, satisfying\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\operatorname{val}(B)\n-\\left(1-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{val}(H)}4\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le\\tau/4. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nBinary translation constraints remain binary translations.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\tau\\), the construction is polynomial-time in the explicit encoding of vertices, occurrences, permutation tables, and binary rational weights.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the weights as \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_h\\). Start with \\(n_e=\\lfloor Qw_e\\rfloor\\); add one to the \\(Q-\\sum_e\\lfloor Qw_e\\rfloor\\) largest fractional parts, breaking ties deterministically. Then\n\\[\nn_e\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_e n_e=Q,\\qquad |w_e-n_e/Q|\\le1/Q.\n\\]\nThe differences sum to zero, so every satisfied-occurrence set changes weight by at most\n\\[\n\\frac12\\sum_e|w_e-n_e/Q|\n\\le\\frac h{2Q}\\le\\tau.\n\\]\nReplace occurrence \\(e\\) by \\(n_e\\) copies, obtaining an unweighted multigraph \\(H'\\) with\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{val}(H')-\\operatorname{val}(H)|\\le\\tau.\n\\]</p><p>Orient an occurrence from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\), with \\(a_v=\\rho_e(a_u)\\), and replace it by\n\\[\nu-p_e-q_e-r_e-v,\n\\]\nusing three new interior vertices per occurrence. Put originals and \\(q_e\\) on the left, and \\(p_e,r_e\\) on the right. In the path direction use identity constraints on the first three edges and \\(\\rho_e\\) on the last; invert permutations when orienting edges left-to-right.</p><p>For fixed original labels, a satisfied occurrence extends to four satisfied edges. A violated one cannot extend to four but extends to exactly three by propagating along the first three. Interiors are disjoint, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(B)\n=1-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{val}(H')}{4}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nThis proves (7.1).</p><p>A loop becomes a four-cycle. Parallel occurrences have separate interiors, so the graph is simple and bipartite. Identities and inverses preserve binary translation form.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\tau\\), \\(Q\\le h/\\tau+1\\) is polynomially bounded. Exact division and comparison of rational remainders are polynomial in bit complexity, as is listing the new graph.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma A.1 (Simple strong-left games), in its normalization-and-transformation form", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nIn a bipartite Unique Games instance oriented left-to-right, a left vertex is strongly satisfied if every incident edge is satisfied.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\zeta,\\nu&lt;1\\) and integers \\(d,H\\ge1\\). There exist an integer \\(h\\ge H\\) divisible by \\(d\\), and positive rational errors \\(\\zeta_0,\\gamma_0&lt;1/2\\), chosen independently of the alphabet, with the following property.</p><p>Any nonempty explicit unweighted simple bipartite Unique Games instance \\(\\Phi_0\\) over \\([R]\\) can be transformed deterministically, in polynomial time for these fixed parameters, to a nonempty explicit unweighted simple bipartite game \\(\\Phi=(A,B,E)\\) over the same alphabet, of common left degree \\(h\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li\">a labeling of \\(\\Phi_0\\) of edge value at least \\(1-\\zeta_0\\) yields a labeling strongly satisfying more than \\(1-\\zeta\\) of the left vertices of \\(\\Phi\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi_0)\\le\\gamma_0\\) implies \\(\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)&lt;\\nu\\).</p><p>Consequently, applying this transformation to a reduction with the promises of Theorem 1 at errors \\(\\zeta_0,\\gamma_0\\) gives the \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) reduction asserted by the paper’s Lemma A.1. The degree and required errors are fixed before that reduction supplies the alphabet.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adapt [27, Lemmas 3.3, 3.4, and 3.6], adding right-vertex tags for simplicity.</p><p>Choose a multiple \\(h\\) of \\(d\\) with\n\\[\nh\\ge\\max\\{H,2\\},\\qquad 1/h&lt;\\nu/4.\n\\]\nChoose fixed \\(T\\ge h\\) with\n\\[\np_{\\mathrm{graph}}=(T)_h/T^h&gt;3/4;\n\\]\nthis is possible since \\(1-(T)_h/T^h\\le\\binom h2/T\\). Choose positive rational \\(\\beta,\\gamma,u\\) and integer \\(L_2\\) so that\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;\\nu/4,\\quad h^2\\gamma&lt;\\nu/4,\\quad\n(h+1)u&lt;\\zeta/2,\\quad\n1/L_2&lt;\\min\\{\\beta\\gamma/2,\\zeta/(2h)\\}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\gamma_0,\\zeta_0\\in(0,1/2)\\) rational with\n\\[\n2\\gamma_0&lt;\\beta\\gamma/2,\\qquad \\sqrt{2\\zeta_0}&lt;u.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(\\Phi_0=(X_0,Y,E_0)\\), with \\(M_0=|E_0|&gt;0\\), uniform edge weights, and total incident weight \\(w_x\\) at \\(x\\). Create\n\\[\nn_x=\\lfloor2|X_0|w_x\\rfloor\n\\]\ncopies of \\(x\\), each with local weights \\(w_{xy}/w_x\\); only positive-\\(w_x\\) vertices have copies. If \\(X_1\\) is the copy set,\n\\[\n|X_0|\\le|X_1|=\\sum_xn_x\\le2|X_0|.\n\\]\nGiven any copy-game labeling, keep right labels and optimize the common local objective at each original \\(x\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{x'\\in X_1}w_{\\mathrm{sat}}(x')\n\\le\n\\sum_{x:n_x&gt;0}\\frac{n_x}{w_x}w_{\\mathrm{sat},L}(x)\n\\le2|X_0|w_{\\mathrm{sat},L}(\\Phi_0).\n\\]\nThe normalized NO value is at most \\(2\\gamma_0\\).</p><p>For a YES labeling, the corresponding calculation bounds mean local loss by \\(2\\zeta_0&lt;u^2\\). Markov leaves more than \\(1-u\\) of the copies with local satisfied weight at least \\(1-u\\).</p><p>Put \\(\\alpha=L_2|Y|\\). At each left copy, round \\(\\alpha\\) times every local weight except one chosen neighbor down, and give the remaining occurrences to that neighbor. This produces a left-\\(\\alpha\\)-regular multigraph \\(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{occ}}\\). Every subset of neighbors changes weight by at most \\(1/L_2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{occ}})\n\\le2\\gamma_0+1/L_2&lt;\\beta\\gamma \\tag{A.1}\n\\]\non NO, while more than \\(1-u\\) of left copies have satisfied fraction at least \\(1-a\\), where \\(a=u+1/L_2\\), on YES.</p><p>Index the occurrences at \\(x\\) by \\(j\\in[\\alpha]\\), with endpoints \\(y_j\\) and permutations \\(\\pi_j\\). Replace \\(y\\) by \\((y,t)\\), \\(t\\in[T]\\), and each occurrence by all its tagged copies, obtaining \\(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{tag}}\\). For any labeling, randomly choosing one tag label for each original \\(y\\) gives in expectation its tagged edge value in \\(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{occ}}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{tag}})\n\\le\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi_{\\mathrm{occ}})&lt;\\beta\\gamma. \\tag{A.2}\n\\]\nThe tag-independent YES labeling preserves local satisfied fractions.</p><p>Make full sequence vertices\n\\[\n(x,(j_1,t_1),\\ldots,(j_h,t_h)),\n\\]\nwith one edge at each position \\(i\\) to \\((y_{j_i},t_i)\\), carrying \\(\\pi_{j_i}\\). Each \\(x\\) contributes equally many vertices, and the edge value is the expected satisfied fraction of \\(h\\) independent occurrences.</p><p>Fix right labels. Each occurrence determines a unique satisfying left label. Let \\(p_x(\\ell)\\) be its distribution. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_x\\max_\\ell p_x(\\ell)&lt;\\beta\\gamma.\n\\]\nFewer than a \\(\\beta\\) fraction of \\(x\\) have maximum at least \\(\\gamma\\). At every other \\(x\\), the probability of a collision among \\(h\\) determined labels is at most\n\\[\n\\binom h2\\sum_\\ell p_x(\\ell)^2&lt;h^2\\gamma.\n\\]\nWithout a collision, any sequence-dependent left label satisfies at most one edge. Therefore the full sequence graph has value at most\n\\[\ns=\\beta+1/h+(1-\\beta)h^2\\gamma&lt;3\\nu/4. \\tag{A.3}\n\\]</p><p>Retain only vertices with distinct tags \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_h\\). Each \\(x\\) and each base occurrence sequence retain exactly \\((T)_h\\) tag sequences, a fraction \\(p_{\\mathrm{graph}}\\). The right neighbors of every retained left vertex are distinct, so the graph is simple and left-\\(h\\)-regular.</p><p>For YES, tags do not affect satisfaction. A left copy of satisfied fraction \\(f_x\\) gives strongly satisfied fraction \\(f_x^h\\). Thus the overall strongly satisfied fraction is at least\n\\[\n(1-u)(1-a)^h\n\\ge1-(h+1)u-h/L_2&gt;1-\\zeta.\n\\]\nFor NO, extend any retained labeling arbitrarily to discarded sequence vertices. Its retained edge value is at most\n\\[\ns/p_{\\mathrm{graph}}&lt;\\nu.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(N\\) bounds the original explicit size, then \\(|X_1|=O(N)\\), \\(\\alpha=O(N)\\), and there are\n\\[\n|X_1|\\alpha^h(T)_h=O(N^{h+1})\n\\]\nretained left vertices, each of degree \\(h\\). Identifiers, rounding arithmetic, and permutation tables have polynomial encoding size.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition A.2 (Finite ordering transfer)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(\\varnothing\\ne\\Pi\\subsetneq S_k\\), \\(\\rho_\\Pi=|\\Pi|/k!\\), and \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;(1-\\rho_\\Pi)/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an alphabet \\([R]\\), let \\(\\Phi=(A,B,E)\\) be a nonempty explicit unweighted simple bipartite Unique Games instance, oriented from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\), with common left degree \\(h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An actual ordering-CSP occurrence must have distinct variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist integers \\(q,H\\ge1\\) and positive rationals \\(\\zeta,\\nu&lt;1\\), depending only on \\(k,\\Pi,\\delta\\), such that whenever \\(h\\ge H\\) and \\(q\\mid h\\), a deterministic transformation produces a finite rationally weighted \\(\\Pi\\)-ordering-CSP instance \\(I\\) satisfying:\n\\[\n\\text{at least }(1-\\zeta)|A|\\text{ strongly satisfied left vertices}\n\\implies \\operatorname{OPT}_\\Pi(I)\\ge1-\\delta,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\le\\nu\n\\implies \\operatorname{OPT}_\\Pi(I)\\le\\rho_\\Pi+\\delta.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(R\\), the transformation is polynomial in the explicit size of \\(\\Phi\\). The constants \\(q,H,\\zeta,\\nu\\) are independent of \\(R,h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize the predicate using \\(P_0\\) from Lemma 12.1. For a raw tuple \\(w=(w_1,\\ldots,w_k)\\), emit \\(v\\) by\n\\[\nv_{\\pi_0(j)}=w_j. \\tag{A.5}\n\\]\nIf \\(w\\) induces permutation \\(\\sigma\\), then \\(v\\) induces \\(\\pi_0\\circ\\sigma\\). This preserves payoff, including the uniform tie extension, and preserves distinctness and weights.</p><p>First derive estimates for every \\(R\\). Fix \\(0&lt;\\kappa&lt;1/2\\), \\(q\\) with \\(1/q\\le\\mu_0(\\kappa)\\), local data from Lemma 12.1, and \\(0&lt;t&lt;1/(kq)\\), \\(\\chi&gt;0\\). Use Lemma 12.3’s experiment with resampling probability \\(2\\kappa\\).</p><p>For edge \\(ab\\), define its action on strings by\n\\[\n(\\pi_{a\\to b}z)_{\\pi_{a\\to b}(\\ell)}=z_\\ell.\n\\]\nThe output variable set is \\(V=B\\times[m]^R\\). In the independent-neighbor experiment, choose uniform \\(a\\), choose \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_k\\) independently uniformly from \\(N(a)\\), and choose the local noisy strings independently of these neighbors. Raw positions are\n\\[\nw_i=(b_i,\\pi_{a\\to b_i}\\widetilde Z_i). \\tag{A.14}\n\\]\nFor any integer rank map \\(f\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(f)=\\mathbb E\\overline P_0(f(w_1),\\ldots,f(w_k)),\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal V_a(f)\\) be the conditional value at \\(a\\). Ties are allowed at this stage, even if output IDs repeat.</p><p>If \\(L\\) strongly satisfies \\(1-\\zeta\\) of the left vertices, use \\(f(b,z)=z_{L(b)}\\). At strongly satisfied \\(a\\),\n\\[\nf(b,\\pi_{a\\to b}z)=z_{L(a)}\n\\]\nfor every neighbor. If none of the \\(k\\) read symbols is resampled, their tuple has law \\(\\mu_c\\). Nonnegativity yields\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(f)\\ge(1-\\zeta)(1-2\\kappa)^k v_{\\mathrm{loc}}. \\tag{A.15}\n\\]</p><p>For soundness, fix an injective global order \\(\\mathcal O:V\\to[|V|]\\). At each \\(a\\), sort the \\(hm^R\\) distinct IDs in \\(N(a)\\times[m]^R\\) and split them into \\(q\\) consecutive equal blocks \\(C_{a,j}\\). This is possible because \\(q\\mid h\\). Define\n\\[\nF_a^j(z)=\\frac1h\\sum_{b\\in N(a)}\n1_{\\{(b,\\pi_{a\\to b}z)\\in C_{a,j}\\}}. \\tag{A.16}\n\\]\nThen \\(F_a(z)\\in S_q\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_zF_a^j(z)=1/q. \\tag{A.17}\n\\]</p><p>For any interval \\(I\\) of global ranks define\n\\[\nF_a^I(z)=\\frac1h\\sum_{b\\in N(a)}\n1_{\\{\\mathcal O(b,\\pi_{a\\to b}z)\\in I\\}}.\n\\]\nCall \\(a\\) pseudorandom if\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(T_{1-\\kappa}F_a^I)\\le t\n\\]\nfor every \\(I,\\ell\\). In particular the bound holds for each block function.</p><p>Conditional on the query strings, independent neighbors give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{b_1,\\ldots,b_k}\n\\overline P_0(j_a(w_1),\\ldots,j_a(w_k))\n=P_0^{\\mathrm{ml}}(F_a(\\widetilde Z_1),\\ldots,F_a(\\widetilde Z_k)). \\tag{A.18}\n\\]\nFurther smoothing decreases influences, so Lemma 12.3 bounds coarse payoff at a pseudorandom \\(a\\) by \\(\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)+r_s(kqt)\\).</p><p>Fine and coarse induced permutations agree when the coarse block ranks are distinct. For two positions, their block-tie probability is\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^q\n\\mathbb E_c\\mathbb E_{\\mu_c^{\\otimes R}}\n[(T_{1-2\\kappa}F_a^j)(Z_i)\n (T_{1-2\\kappa}F_a^j)(Z_{i'})].\n\\]\nConditional on \\(c\\), each marginal is uniform product measure. Lemma 12.2, at mean \\(1/q\\), bounds this by\n\\[\nq^{-\\kappa/2}+q\\,r_c^{m,\\kappa}(t).\n\\]\nA union bound therefore gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_a(\\mathcal O)\n\\le S_{\\mathrm{loc}}\n:=\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)\n+\\binom k2\\bigl(q^{-\\kappa/2}+q\\,r_c^{m,\\kappa}(t)\\bigr)\n+r_s(kqt) \\tag{A.19}\n\\]\nat every pseudorandom \\(a\\).</p><p>To decode nonpseudorandom vertices, restrict \\(\\mathcal O\\) to each right code \\(\\{b\\}\\times[m]^R\\) and compress ranks to a strict order \\(o_b\\). Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_b=\n\\{\\ell:\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(T_{1-\\kappa}1_{\\{o_b(z)\\in I\\}})\n&gt;t/2\\text{ for some interval }I\\}.\n\\]\nThe few-influential-coordinates lemma [17, Definition 4.2 and Lemma 4.3] gives\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_b|\\le\\frac{3200}{\\kappa t^3}. \\tag{A.20}\n\\]</p><p>For nonpseudorandom \\(a\\), choose a witnessing interval \\(I_a\\) and coordinate \\(\\ell_a\\). Convexity of the squared-norm influence, and commutation of noise with coordinate permutations, give\n\\[\nt&lt;\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{\\ell_a}(T_{1-\\kappa}F_a^{I_a})\n\\le\\frac1h\\sum_{b\\in N(a)}\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{\\pi_{a\\to b}(\\ell_a)}\n(T_{1-\\kappa}1_{\\{\\mathcal O(b,z)\\in I_a\\}}). \\tag{A.21}\n\\]\nEvery summand is at most one, so more than a \\(t/2\\) fraction exceed \\(t/2\\). For these neighbors,\n\\(\\pi_{a\\to b}(\\ell_a)\\in\\mathcal L_b\\).</p><p>Label such \\(a\\) by \\(\\ell_a\\), and independently label each right vertex uniformly from its nonempty \\(\\mathcal L_b\\), arbitrarily if empty. Expected satisfied edge fraction at each such \\(a\\) exceeds\n\\[\n\\frac t2\\frac{\\kappa t^3}{3200}\n=\\frac{\\kappa t^4}{6400}.\n\\]\nOne right labeling is shared by all left vertices. Equal left degrees identify the average with uniform edge value.</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal V(\\mathcal O)&gt;S_{\\mathrm{loc}}+\\chi\\), more than a \\(\\chi\\) fraction of left vertices must be nonpseudorandom. Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\le\\nu&lt;\n\\frac{\\chi\\kappa t^4}{6400}\n\\implies\n\\mathcal V(\\mathcal O)\\le S_{\\mathrm{loc}}+\\chi. \\tag{A.22}\n\\]</p><p>Now condition on distinct neighbors \\(D=\\{b_1,\\ldots,b_k\\text{ distinct}\\}\\). For \\(h\\ge k\\),\n\\[\np_D=(h)_k/h^k,\\qquad\n\\delta_h=1-p_D\\le\\binom k2/h. \\tag{A.23}\n\\]\nIts probability is the same at every \\(a\\), and it is independent of the local experiment. All output IDs are distinct on \\(D\\). For any rank map \\(f\\), conditional payoff \\(\\mathcal V_D(f)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V_D(f)-\\mathcal V(f)|\\le\\delta_h. \\tag{A.24}\n\\]</p><p>Refine the YES coarse map by independently uniformly permuting the distinct IDs within each rank fiber. On any conditioned tuple this realizes the uniform tie extension in expectation; some strict global order attains that expected value. For soundness, an injective order already gives strict payoff. Thus the conditioned \\(P_0\\)-instance satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\operatorname{OPT}\\ge\n(1-\\zeta)(1-2\\kappa)^kv_{\\mathrm{loc}}-\\delta_h,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\operatorname{OPT}\\le\nS_{\\mathrm{loc}}+\\chi+\\delta_h\n\\quad\\text{if }\\nu&lt;\\chi\\kappa t^4/6400. \\tag{A.25}\n\\]\nPermuting tuple positions by (A.5) yields the original predicate \\(\\Pi\\).</p><p>Choose the numerical parameters in this order, independently of \\(R\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. rational \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) with \\(10\\eta&lt;\\delta\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. rational \\(0&lt;\\kappa&lt;1/2\\) with \\(2\\kappa k&lt;\\eta\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. writing \\(K_0=\\binom k2\\), choose \\(q\\) with\n\\[\n1/q\\le\\mu_0(\\kappa),\\qquad K_0q^{-\\kappa/2}&lt;\\eta;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. choose local data from Lemma 12.1 at error \\(\\eta\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">5. choose rational \\(0&lt;t&lt;1/(kq)\\) with\n\\[\nK_0q\\,r_c^{m,\\kappa}(t)&lt;\\eta,\\qquad r_s(kqt)&lt;\\eta;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. set \\(\\chi=\\eta\\), and choose rational \\(\\zeta,\\nu\\in(0,1)\\) with\n\\[\n\\zeta&lt;\\eta,\\qquad \\nu&lt;\\eta\\kappa t^4/6400;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">7. choose \\(H\\ge\\max\\{k,q\\}\\) with \\(K_0/H&lt;\\eta\\).</p><p>For every \\(h\\ge H\\), the YES bound exceeds \\(1-4\\eta&gt;1-\\delta\\), and the NO bound is less than \\(\\rho_\\Pi+6\\eta&lt;\\rho_\\Pi+\\delta\\).</p><p>For explicit encoding, once \\(R\\) is fixed, the local occurrence, product \\(\\mu_c^{\\otimes R}\\), and rational \\(2\\kappa\\)-noise form one fixed finite rational law \\(\\theta_R\\). List \\(B\\times[m]^R\\). For every \\(a\\), ordered distinct neighbor tuple, and local outcome \\(\\xi\\), emit the tuple (A.14), permuted by (A.5), with weight\n\\[\n\\frac{\\theta_R(\\xi)}{|A|(h)_k}. \\tag{A.26}\n\\]\nThese weights sum to one. There are \\(|B|m^R\\) variables and at most \\(|A|(h)_k|\\operatorname{supp}\\theta_R|\\) occurrences. For fixed \\(R\\), these counts are polynomial, and weights have \\(O(\\log|A|+k\\log h)\\) bits plus a fixed constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma A.3 (Rational local ordering data)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nChoose \\(\\pi_0\\in\\Pi\\), put \\(P=1_\\Pi\\), and define\n\\[\nP_0(\\sigma)=P(\\pi_0\\circ\\sigma),\\qquad \\sigma\\in S_k.\n\\]\nThen \\(P_0(\\operatorname{id})=1\\), and its average is \\(\\rho_\\Pi\\).</p><p>For integer ranks \\(r=(r_1,\\ldots,r_k)\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Ref}(r)\\) be permutations listing positions in increasing rank, breaking ties arbitrarily, and define\n\\[\n\\overline P_0(r)\n=\\frac1{|\\operatorname{Ref}(r)|}\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Ref}(r)}P_0(\\sigma). \\tag{A.4}\n\\]\nFor a finite weighted instance \\(J=(U,\\mathcal C,\\omega)\\), write its occurrence tuple as \\((u_{c,1},\\ldots,u_{c,k})\\), and set\n\\[\n\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)=\n\\max_{g:U\\to[q]}\n\\sum_c\\omega_c\n\\overline P_0(g(u_{c,1}),\\ldots,g(u_{c,k})).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(q\\ge1\\) and rational \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there are a finite local instance \\(J\\), \\(|U|=m\\ge2\\), and laws \\(\\mu_c\\) on \\([m]^k\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li\">every occurrence has distinct variables;</p><p class=\"li\">all weights and atoms are rational;</p><p class=\"li\">every one-position marginal is uniform;</p><p class=\"li\">some fixed \\(0&lt;\\alpha\\le1/2\\) satisfies \\(\\mu_c(x)&gt;\\alpha\\) for every \\(c,x\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\n\\[\n\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)\\le\\rho_\\Pi+\\eta,\\qquad\nv_{\\mathrm{loc}}:=\\sum_c\\omega_c\\mathbb E_{\\mu_c}\\overline P_0(X)\\ge1-\\eta; \\tag{A.6}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">there are common feasible Gram data: a unit vector \\(I\\) and vectors \\(b_{u,j}\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_jb_{u,j}=I,\\quad\n\\langle b_{u,i},b_{u,j}\\rangle=0\\ (i\\ne j),\\quad\n\\|b_{u,j}\\|^2=1/m,\n\\]\nand for all positions \\(r,s\\) of \\(c\\),\n\\[\n\\langle b_{u_{c,r},i},b_{u_{c,s},j}\\rangle\n=\\Pr_{\\mu_c}[X_r=i,X_s=j]. \\tag{A.7}\n\\]\nAll data depend only on \\(k,\\Pi,q,\\eta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The increasing-tuple construction [17, Theorem 11.1, Definition 11.2, and proof of Theorem 2.5], applied to \\(P_0\\), gives a finite \\(J^*\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{opt}_q(J^*)\\le\\rho_\\Pi+\\eta\n\\]\nand a linear order satisfying every constraint. Its finite uniform choices give rational weights. Tuple positions lie in successive disjoint intervals, so variables are distinct.</p><p>Use the finite-copy smoothing from [17, Lemma 8.9]. Take \\(L\\) disjoint copies of \\(J^*\\), each of weight \\(1/L\\), and concatenate their satisfying orders into \\(\\sigma:U\\to[m]\\), where \\(m=L|U^*|\\). The bound on \\(\\operatorname{opt}_q\\) persists by averaging over copies.</p><p>Choose \\(L\\) and rational \\(s_0\\in(0,1)\\) with \\(1/L+s_0&lt;\\eta\\). With probability \\(1-s_0\\), choose a uniform cyclic shift of \\(\\sigma\\)’s ranks; otherwise assign every variable independently uniformly from \\([m]\\). Each variable is uniform. A cyclic cut splits at most one copy, so every shift has payoff at least \\(1-1/L\\). The mixture has payoff greater than \\(1-\\eta\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mu_c\\) be its restriction to occurrence \\(c\\). If \\(n_c(x)\\) counts shifts with restriction \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\mu_c(x)=(1-s_0)\\frac{n_c(x)}m+\\frac{s_0}{m^k}.\n\\]\nThese atoms are rational and exceed \\(\\alpha=s_0/(2m^k)\\).</p><p>On this finite probability space, take \\(I=1\\) and \\(b_{u,j}=1_{\\{X(u)=j\\}}\\) in \\(L^2\\). Uniform marginals, disjoint labels, and restrictions to each occurrence give the Gram identities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma A.4 (Uniform collision estimate)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nFor scalar \\(F:[m]^R\\to\\mathbb R\\), under uniform product measure, set\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(F)\n=\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}_{z_\\ell}(F\\mid z_{-\\ell})].\n\\]\nThe operator \\(T_\\theta\\) independently retains each coordinate with probability \\(\\theta\\), otherwise uniformly resampling it, and averages the function. In an orthonormal product expansion,\n\\[\nT_\\theta F=\\sum_\\sigma\\theta^{|\\sigma|}\\widehat F(\\sigma)\\chi_\\sigma,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(F)=\n\\sum_{\\sigma:\\sigma_\\ell\\ne0}\\widehat F(\\sigma)^2. \\tag{A.8}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0&lt;\\kappa&lt;1/2\\), there is \\(\\mu_0(\\kappa)&gt;0\\). For every \\(m\\ge2\\), there is a nonnegative function \\(r_c^{m,\\kappa}(t)\\to0\\) as \\(t\\downarrow0\\), such that, uniformly over \\(R\\ge1\\), if \\(F,G:[m]^R\\to[0,1]\\) have common mean \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le\\mu_0(\\kappa)\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(T_{1-\\kappa}F),\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(T_{1-\\kappa}G)\\le t\n\\quad(\\ell\\in[R]),\n\\]\nthen for every coupling \\((Z,Z')\\) with uniform product marginals,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(T_{1-2\\kappa}F)(Z)(T_{1-2\\kappa}G)(Z')]\n\\le\\mu^{1+\\kappa/2}+r_c^{m,\\kappa}(t). \\tag{A.9}\n\\]\nThe error is independent of \\(R,\\mu,F,G\\), and of the coupling.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(H=T_{1-\\kappa}F\\), the Fourier formula gives\n\\[\n\\|T_{1-2\\kappa}F\\|_2^2\n\\le\\operatorname{Stab}_{1-\\kappa}(H),\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\n(1-\\kappa)^3-(1-2\\kappa)^2\n=\\kappa(1-\\kappa-\\kappa^2)&gt;0.\n\\]\nFull influence bounds imply the low-degree bounds in [32, Theorem 4.4]. With atom bound \\(1/m\\) and correlation \\(1-\\kappa\\), that theorem gives, for sufficiently small \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Stab}_{1-\\kappa}(H)\n\\le\\Gamma_{1-\\kappa}(\\mu)\n+C\\frac{\\log(2m)}{\\kappa}\n\\frac{\\log\\log(1/t)}{\\log(1/t)}.\n\\]\nThe cutoff and error are independent of \\(R,\\mu\\).</p><p>By [32, Theorem B.5],\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{1-\\kappa}(\\mu)\n=\\mu^{2/(2-\\kappa)+o(1)}\n\\quad(\\mu\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac2{2-\\kappa}-\\left(1+\\frac\\kappa2\\right)\n=\\frac{\\kappa^2}{2(2-\\kappa)}&gt;0,\n\\]\nchoose \\(\\mu_0(\\kappa)\\) so that\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{1-\\kappa}(\\mu)\\le\\mu^{1+\\kappa/2}.\n\\]\nUse the displayed error below a suitable \\(t_0&lt;e^{-e}\\), and set the error to one for \\(t\\ge t_0\\), where the estimate is trivial. The same squared \\(L^2\\) estimate holds for \\(G\\). Cauchy–Schwarz under any coupling with the stated marginals proves (A.9).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Lemma A.5 (A simplex-valued local estimate)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.\nUse \\(J,\\mu_c,\\alpha,m\\) from Lemma 12.1. Let\n\\[\nS_q=\\{p\\in[0,1]^q:\\sum_jp_j=1\\},\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nP_0^{\\mathrm{ml}}(p_1,\\ldots,p_k)\n=\\sum_{r\\in[q]^k}\\overline P_0(r)\\prod_{i=1}^k p_i^{r_i}. \\tag{A.10}\n\\]\nFix \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\).</p><p>Sample \\(c\\) with probability \\(\\omega_c\\), then sample the \\(R\\) coordinate tuples independently from \\(\\mu_c\\), giving strings \\(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_k\\in[m]^R\\). Obtain \\(\\widetilde Z_i\\) by independent uniform resampling of every position and coordinate with probability \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Let one function \\(F=(F^1,\\ldots,F^q):[m]^R\\to S_q\\) be used at every occurrence and position, and assume\n\\[\n0&lt;t&lt;1/(kq),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell(T_{1-\\lambda}F^j)\\le t\n\\quad(\\ell\\in[R],j\\in[q]).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E_{c,Z,\\widetilde Z}\nP_0^{\\mathrm{ml}}(F(\\widetilde Z_1),\\ldots,F(\\widetilde Z_k))\n\\le\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)+r_s(kqt), \\tag{A.11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_s(u)\\to0\\) as \\(u\\downarrow0\\), independently of \\(R\\). The error depends only on the fixed local data, \\(k,q,\\lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(H=T_{1-\\lambda}F\\). Independence of the noise between positions and multilinearity turn the left-hand side into\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_c\\mathbb E_{Z\\sim\\mu_c^{\\otimes R}}\nP_0^{\\mathrm{ml}}(H(Z_1),\\ldots,H(Z_k)). \\tag{A.12}\n\\]</p><p>For each coordinate, use the integral indicator ensemble under \\(\\mu_c\\), whose atoms exceed \\(\\alpha\\). The common Gram data give one Gaussian ensemble for all variables of \\(J\\), matching the first and second moments on each constraint. Explicitly, with standard Gaussian \\(g\\) in the Gram-vector span, set\n\\[\nG_{u,j}\n=\\langle I,b_{u,j}\\rangle+\n\\langle b_{u,j}-\\langle I,b_{u,j}\\rangle I,g\\rangle.\n\\]\nUse independent copies across the \\(R\\) coordinates, and evaluate each \\(H^j\\) through its multilinear product expansion.</p><p>Let \\(T_\\theta^c\\) retain a whole coordinate tuple with probability \\(\\theta\\), otherwise resampling it from \\(\\mu_c\\). Uniform marginals give\n\\[\nT_{1-\\lambda}^c[F^j(Z_i)]\n=(T_{1-\\lambda}F^j)(Z_i)=H^j(Z_i).\n\\]\nThey also give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell^{\\mu_c^{\\otimes R}}(H^j(Z_i))\n=\\operatorname{Inf}_\\ell^{[m]^R}(H^j). \\tag{A.13}\n\\]\nThe vector of all \\(kq\\) components has each coordinate influence at most \\(kqt\\), and each component variance at most one.</p><p>For the required tail condition, tuple noise multiplies each Hoeffding component indexed by \\(S\\subseteq[R]\\) by \\((1-\\lambda)^{|S|}\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}((H^j(Z_i))^{&gt;d})\n\\le(1-\\lambda)^{2d}\\operatorname{Var}(F^j(Z_i))\n\\le(1-\\lambda)^{2d}.\n\\]\nThese are the hypotheses of [21, Theorem 3.6] and the vector-valued invariance estimate [17, Theorem 10.1].</p><p>Let \\(p:\\mathbb R^q\\to S_q\\) be Euclidean projection. It is nonexpansive, and\n\\[\n\\Psi(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)\n=P_0^{\\mathrm{ml}}(p(x_1),\\ldots,p(x_k))\n\\]\nhas Lipschitz constant at most \\(\\sqrt{kq}\\): telescope over positions, bound by \\(\\sum_i\\|x_i-y_i\\|_1\\), and apply Cauchy–Schwarz. The invariance error is at most \\(r_s(kqt)\\), with\n\\[\nr_s(u)=C_{kq}\\sqrt{kq}\\,\nu^{\\lambda/(18\\log(1/\\alpha))},\n\\]\nindependent of \\(R\\).</p><p>On the integral ensemble, \\(H(Z_i)\\in S_q\\), so \\(\\Psi\\) is the payoff in (A.12). On the common Gaussian ensemble, project \\(H(G_u)\\) to \\(S_q\\) and independently draw one label per local variable. This is one random assignment of \\(J\\). Distinct variables within each tuple make its conditional expected payoff exactly \\(\\Psi\\). Every realized assignment has value at most \\(\\operatorname{opt}_q(J)\\). Average over \\(c\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [0, 28, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 16, "cite"], [5, 18, "cite"], [5, 19, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 8, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 5, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 5, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 25, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 26, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 20, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [18, 26, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 25, "cite"], [20, 26, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 20, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [28, 1, "cite"], [28, 2, "cite"], [28, 3, "cite"], [29, 3, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 32, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 29, "cite"]]});
